  <section*|Introduction>

  Constructive quantum field theory aims at constructing, with full
  mathematical rigour, examples of <em|interacting> relativistic quantum
  fields, i.e. models satisfying a small set of axioms which encode quantum
  mechanics, special relativity and causality. In spacetime dimension
  <math|d=2> and <math|d=3> this programme has been carried out for several
  models (<math|P<around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsub|2>>, Yukawa<rsub|<math|2>>,
  <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>, sine-Gordon, ...), while in <math|d=4> no
  interacting model is known, and the scaling limits of critical lattice
  <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|4>> models are Gaussian
  (trivial)<nbsp><cite|ADC21>.

  The main lesson of the late 1960s and 1970s<nbsp><cite|Sym69|Nel73a|Nel73b|OS73|OS75|GRS75>
  is that it is much more efficient to work in <em|imaginary time>. The
  relativistic theory is then encoded in a probability measure on
  distributions on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>, which looks like the continuum
  limit of a classical lattice spin system at a critical point. All modern
  approaches (cluster expansions, renormalisation group, stochastic
  quantisation, variational methods) work at this Euclidean level.

  <paragraph|Plan.> Section<nbsp><reference|sec:wightman> recalls the
  Minkowski picture: Wightman axioms, the need for distributions, the
  Källén\ULehmann representation, the quantum wave equation.
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:euclid> explains the passage to imaginary time
  on a lattice regularisation of the field on a circle, the Feynman\UKac
  formula, the statistical mechanics interpretation and the
  Osterwalder\USchrader axioms. Lecture 2
  (Section<nbsp><hlink|3|cqft_notes_L2.tm#sec:gff> of its notes) studies the
  first example, the Gaussian free field. Section<nbsp><reference|app:plan>
  outlines the first three lectures of the course.

  <paragraph|Conventions.> <math|\<hbar\>=c=1>. Spacetime dimension is
  <math|d=1+s>, <math|s\<geq\>1> the number of space dimensions. Inner
  products <math|<around|\<langle\>|f,g|\<rangle\>>=<big|int><wide|f|\<bar\>>*g>
  are antilinear in the first slot. For <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|)>>,
  <math|<wide|f|^><around|(|k|)>=<big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>k\<cdot\>x>f<around|(|x|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>x>.
  Constants <math|C> may change from line to line.

  <section|Relativistic quantum fields in Minkowski
  space><label|sec:wightman>

  <subsection|Kinematics>

  Minkowski space is <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|1,s>\<simeq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>
  with points <math|x=<around|(|x<rsup|0>,<math-bf|x>|)>> and bilinear form
  <math|x\<cdot\>y=x<rsup|0>*y<rsup|0>-<math-bf|x>\<cdot\><math-bf|y>>,
  <math|x<rsup|2>=x\<cdot\>x>. Two points are <em|spacelike separated> if
  <math|<around|(|x-y|)><rsup|2>\<less\>0>. The open forward light cone is
  <math|V<rsub|<space|-0.17em>+>=<around|{|p:<space|0.17em>p<rsup|0>\<gtr\><around|\||<math-bf|p>|\|>|}>>,
  with closure <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|<space|-0.17em>+>>. The restricted
  Lorentz group <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|+>> is the connected
  component of the identity of the group of linear maps preserving
  <math|x\<cdot\>y>; the restricted Poincaré group is
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|+>=\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>\<rtimes\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|+>>,
  with <math|<around|(|a,\<Lambda\>|)><around|(|b,\<Lambda\><rprime|'>|)>=<around|(|a+\<Lambda\>*b,\<Lambda\>*\<Lambda\><rprime|'>|)>>,
  acting on spacetime by <math|x\<mapsto\>\<Lambda\>*x+a>.

