  </script-output|<image|<tuple|<#>|ps>||||>><label|fig:cone>>|Minkowski
  space for <math|s=1>. The shaded regions are timelike to the origin. The
  supports of <math|f> and <math|g> are spacelike separated, so
  <math|<around|[|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>,\<varphi\><around|(|g|)>|]>=0> by
  (W3). In momentum space the same forward cone
  <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|<space|-0.17em>+>> carries the joint spectrum
  of <math|<around|(|H,<math-bf|P>|)>>.>

  By Wigner's theorem a relativistic quantum system carries a (projective)
  unitary representation of <math|\<cal-P\><rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|+>> on its
  Hilbert space of states. For a scalar field we may assume a true
  representation.

  <subsection|The Wightman axioms>

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Wightman QFT of a hermitian scalar field><label|def:wightman> A
    Wightman theory is given by the following data, subject to the following
    conditions.

    <\description-long>
      <item*|(W0) Relativistic quantum theory>A separable Hilbert space
      <math|\<cal-H\>> and a strongly continuous unitary representation
      <math|U> of <math|\<cal-P\><rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|+>> on
      <math|\<cal-H\>>. Write <math|U*<around|(|a,<with|math-font|Bbb|1>|)>=\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>P\<cdot\>a>=\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\><around|(|P<rsup|0>*a<rsup|0>-<math-bf|P>\<cdot\><math-bf|a>|)>>>
      for commuting self-adjoint generators
      <math|P=<around|(|P<rsup|0>,<math-bf|P>|)>>;
      <math|H\<assign\>P<rsup|0>> is the Hamiltonian.

      <em|Spectrum condition:> the joint spectrum of
      <math|<around|(|P<rsup|0>,\<ldots\>,P<rsup|s>|)>> is contained in
      <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|<space|-0.17em>+>>; in particular
      <math|H\<geq\><around|\||<math-bf|P>|\|>\<geq\>0>.

      <em|Vacuum:> there is a unit vector <math|\<Omega\>>, unique up to a
      phase among the translation invariant vectors, with
      <math|U*<around|(|a,\<Lambda\>|)>*\<Omega\>=\<Omega\>> for all
      <math|<around|(|a,\<Lambda\>|)>\<in\>\<cal-P\><rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|+>>.

      <item*|(W1) Fields>There is a dense subspace
      <math|\<cal-D\>\<subset\>\<cal-H\>> and, for every
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>|)>>, an operator
      <math|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>> with domain <math|\<cal-D\>> such that
      <math|\<Omega\>\<in\>\<cal-D\>>, <math|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>*\<cal-D\>\<subset\>\<cal-D\>>,
      <math|U*<around|(|a,\<Lambda\>|)>*\<cal-D\>\<subset\>\<cal-D\>>; for
      <math|\<chi\>,\<psi\>\<in\>\<cal-D\>> the map
      <math|f\<mapsto\><around|\<langle\>|\<chi\>,\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>*\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>>
      is a tempered distribution; and <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<chi\>,\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>*\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|\<varphi\><around|(|<wide|f|\<bar\>>|)>*\<chi\>,\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>>.

      <item*|(W2) Covariance><math|U*<around|(|a,\<Lambda\>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>*U*<around|(|a,\<Lambda\>|)><rsup|-1>=\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|<around|(|a,\<Lambda\>|)>>|)>>
      on <math|\<cal-D\>>, where <math|f<rsub|<around|(|a,\<Lambda\>|)>><around|(|x|)>=f*<around|(|\<Lambda\><rsup|-1>*<around|(|x-a|)>|)>>.

      <item*|(W3) Locality>If the supports of <math|f> and <math|g> are
      spacelike separated, then <math|<around|[|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>,\<varphi\><around|(|g|)>|]>*\<psi\>=0>
      for all <math|\<psi\>\<in\>\<cal-D\>>.

      <item*|(W4) Cyclicity of the vacuum>The span of
      <math|<around|{|\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|1>|)>*\<cdots\>*\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|n>|)>*\<Omega\>:<space|0.17em>n\<geq\>0,f<rsub|j>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>|)>|}>>
      is dense in <math|\<cal-H\>>.
    </description-long>
  </definition>

  Formally <math|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>=<big|int>\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>*f<around|(|x|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>x>,
  and <math|\<varphi\>*<around|(|x<rsup|0>,<math-bf|x>|)>=\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>H*x<rsup|0>>\<varphi\>*<around|(|0,<math-bf|x>|)>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>H*x<rsup|0>>>
  is the Heisenberg-picture field. For <math|d\<geq\>3>, Lorentz invariance
  of the vacuum follows from translation invariance and uniqueness:
  <math|U*<around|(|0,\<Lambda\>|)>*\<Omega\>> is translation invariant,
  hence equal to <math|\<chi\><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*\<Omega\>> for a
  character <math|\<chi\>> of <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|+>>, and
  <math|\<chi\>\<equiv\>1> because <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|+>>
  has no nontrivial one-dimensional unitary representations. For <math|d=2>
  the boosts form the abelian group <math|\<bbb-R\>>; there one may always
  replace <math|U*<around|(|a,\<Lambda\>|)>> by
  <math|\<chi\><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)><rsup|-1>*U*<around|(|a,\<Lambda\>|)>>,
  which is why we include Lorentz invariance in (W0). The <em|Wightman
  functions>

  <\equation>
    W<rsub|n><around|(|f<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,f<rsub|n>|)>\<assign\><around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|1>|)>*\<cdots\>*\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|n>|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>
  </equation>

  extend, by the Schwartz kernel theorem, to tempered distributions
  <math|W<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d*n>|)>>.
  They satisfy: (a) <em|positivity>: <math|<big|sum><rsub|j,k>W<rsub|j+k>*<around|(|g<rsub|j><rsup|\<ast\>>\<otimes\>g<rsub|k>|)>\<geq\>0>
  for every finite sequence <math|g<rsub|j>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d*j>|)>>,
  with <math|g<rsup|\<ast\>><around|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|j>|)>=<wide|g<around|(|x<rsub|j>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|1>|)>|\<bar\>>>;
  (b) <em|Poincaré invariance>; (c) <em|spectral condition>:
  <math|W<rsub|n><around|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>=w<rsub|n><around|(|x<rsub|1>-x<rsub|2>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n-1>-x<rsub|n>|)>>
  with <math|<wide|w|^><rsub|n>> supported in
  <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|<space|-0.17em>+><rsup|<space|0.17em>n-1>> (for
  a suitable sign convention); (d) <em|locality>: <math|W<rsub|n>> is
  unchanged under exchange of two adjacent spacelike separated arguments; (e)
  <em|cluster property>, equivalent to uniqueness of the vacuum
  (Remark<nbsp><reference|rem:clustering>).

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Wightman reconstruction<nbsp><cite|SW64>>Any sequence
    <math|<around|(|W<rsub|n>|)><rsub|n\<geq\>0>> of tempered distributions
    with <math|W<rsub|0>=1> satisfying (a)\U(d) is the sequence of Wightman
    functions of a theory satisfying (W1)\U(W4) and (W0) except possibly
    uniqueness of the vacuum, unique up to unitary equivalence. The vacuum is
    unique iff (e) holds.
  </theorem>

  So constructing a QFT amounts to constructing its Wightman functions. For
  the axioms, the reconstruction theorem and the algebraic viewpoint
  see<nbsp><cite|SW64|BSZ92|Haa96>. The difficulty is that the positivity
  condition (a) is hard to verify for candidate Wightman functions: it
  involves all <math|W<rsub|n>> at once, and the objects one can actually
  construct live in imaginary time, whereas (a) refers to their real-time
  boundary values. The Euclidean approach of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:euclid> replaces it by reflection positivity,
  which can be checked on regularised (e.g. lattice) approximations and is
  preserved under the limits one has to take. Note also that the axioms say
  nothing about dynamics: there is no Lagrangian, and the interaction is
  hidden in the higher Wightman functions.

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Minimal requirements><label|rem:minimal> The axioms (W0)\U(W4) are
    a minimal transcription of what we expect of any relativistic quantum
    theory with a local quantity. (W0) encodes quantum mechanics (states in a
    Hilbert space), Poincaré symmetry (Wigner's theorem), and stability: the
    energy is bounded below, which together with Lorentz covariance gives the
    spectrum condition, and there is a ground state. (W1) and (W2) require a
    covariant quantity localised in spacetime regions. (W3) is Einstein
    causality: measurements in spacelike separated regions do not interfere.
    (W4) says that the field is complete: all states are produced from the
    vacuum by local operations. Nothing is assumed about dynamics,
    Lagrangians, perturbation theory or particles. These either follow as
    theorems (PCT and the spin\Ustatistics connection<nbsp><cite|SW64>;
    particles and scattering states from an isolated mass shell, by
    Haag\URuelle theory<nbsp><cite|Haa96>) or are additional structure. A
    construction of a relativistic QFT must at least produce a model of these
    axioms, which is why they are our target. They are often supplemented by
    the <em|time-slice axiom> (primitive causality): the fields smeared in an
    arbitrarily thin time slice already generate all operators. It expresses
    the existence of a dynamical law and is the abstract counterpart of a
    well-posed Cauchy problem<nbsp><cite-detail|Haa96|ŸII.1>.
  </remark>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Local algebras and fields><label|rem:nets> For a bounded open
    region <math|O\<subset\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>, let
    <math|\<frak-A\><around|(|O|)>> be the von Neumann algebra generated by
    the bounded functions of the smeared fields
    <math|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>>, <math|f> real with
    <math|<math-up|supp>f\<subset\>O>. (This requires the closures of the
    <math|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>> to be self-adjoint and to commute
    strongly at spacelike separation, which holds in the models we
    construct.) The map <math|O\<mapsto\>\<frak-A\><around|(|O|)>> is a
    <em|Haag\UKastler net><nbsp><cite|HK64|Haa96>. It satisfies isotony,
    <math|\<frak-A\><around|(|O<rsub|1>|)>\<subset\>\<frak-A\><around|(|O<rsub|2>|)>>
    for <math|O<rsub|1>\<subset\>O<rsub|2>>; Einstein causality,
    <math|<around|[|\<frak-A\><around|(|O<rsub|1>|)>,\<frak-A\><around|(|O<rsub|2>|)>|]>=0>
    for spacelike separated regions; covariance,
    <math|U*<around|(|a,\<Lambda\>|)>*\<frak-A\><around|(|O|)>*U*<around|(|a,\<Lambda\>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>=\<frak-A\>*<around|(|\<Lambda\>*O+a|)>>;
    and the spectrum condition, with a vacuum vector. In the algebraic point
    of view the net <em|is> the physical content of the theory: it specifies
    which observables can be measured in each region, and nothing else. A
    field is a choice of coordinates for the net. Different fields can
    generate the same net, and fields that are local relative to each other
    (the Borchers class of <math|\<varphi\>>, which contains for instance all
    Wick polynomials of a free field) describe the same physics, e.g. the
    same scattering matrix. Conversely, fields need not be observable:
    fermion fields anticommute at spacelike separation, and charged fields
    are not invariant under the gauge group.

    We nevertheless need fields, for three reasons. (a) The net refers to
    regions, not to points. Fields provide the pointlike localised quantities
    in which dynamics (field equations, Lagrangians), interactions (local
    products such as <math|:\<varphi\><rsup|4>>:) and the short-distance
    structure (operator product expansion, scaling dimensions) are expressed.
    (b) Correlation functions of fields are the concrete objects that
    constructive methods produce and estimate: the Euclidean measures of
    these lectures. (c) The two descriptions are close but not equivalent.
    The Wightman axioms alone do not guarantee a net of local algebras of
    bounded operators, and a net does not guarantee a Wightman
    field<nbsp><cite-detail|Haa96|ŸIII.1>. Under a phase-space condition,
    Fredenhagen and Hertel recover pointlike localised fields from a net by
    shrinking the regions to points<nbsp><cite|FH81>. In this course a QFT is
    constructed as a Euclidean measure for a field, and its local algebras
    are those generated by the reconstructed field.
  </remark>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|The vacuum, and states without a vacuum><label|rem:hadamard> In
    Minkowski space the vacuum is singled out by symmetry and stability: it
    is the Poincaré invariant state, and by the spectrum condition it has the
    lowest energy in every Lorentz frame. In the Wightman framework its
    existence is an axiom. In the constructive approach it is a result: it
    arises as the limit <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>> of the Euclidean measures
    (Table<nbsp><reference|tab:dictionary>), and its uniqueness is equivalent
    to clustering (Remark<nbsp><reference|rem:clustering>). On a curved
    spacetime, for instance an expanding Friedmann\URobertson\UWalker
    universe, there is no Poincaré group. Without a timelike Killing field
    there is neither a conserved energy nor a preferred vacuum, and the
    notion of particle becomes observer dependent (particle creation in
    expanding universes, and in gravitational collapse, the Hawking effect;
    already in flat space, the Unruh effect shows that the notion of particle
    depends on the observer). The algebraic framework still makes sense: nets
    of local algebras can be defined locally and
    covariantly<nbsp><cite|BFV03>. But the physically acceptable states must
    be selected by a <em|local> condition, the Hadamard condition: at short
    distance, the two-point function has the same singularity as the
    Minkowski vacuum. In <math|d=4> it reads, schematically,

    <\equation*>
      W<rsub|2><around|(|x,y|)>=<frac|1|4*\<pi\><rsup|2>>*<around*|(|<frac|U*<around|(|x,y|)>|\<sigma\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|x,y|)>>+V<around|(|x,y|)>*log
      \<sigma\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|x,y|)>|)>+w<around|(|x,y|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|\<sigma\><around|(|x,y|)>> is the squared geodesic distance,
    taken positive at spacelike separation (so that
    <math|W<rsub|2>=1/<around|(|4*\<pi\><rsup|2>*\<sigma\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>>
    for the massless field in Minkowski space), and
    <math|\<sigma\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|x,y|)>=\<sigma\><around|(|x,y|)>+2\<mathi\>\<varepsilon\>*<around|(|t<around|(|x|)>-t<around|(|y|)>|)>+\<varepsilon\><rsup|2>>,
    <math|\<varepsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0>, implements the positive-frequency
    <math|\<mathi\>\<varepsilon\>> prescription (<math|t> a time function),
    the coefficients <math|U,V> are determined by the metric and the field
    equation, and <math|w> is smooth and state dependent. By Radzikowski's
    theorem this is equivalent to a <em|microlocal spectrum condition>: the
    wave front set of <math|W<rsub|2>> contains only positive
    frequencies<nbsp><cite|Rad96>. This is the local substitute for
    positivity of the energy. Hadamard states are the states in which Wick
    powers and the expectation of the stress\Uenergy tensor can be
    renormalised, by subtracting the geometric part of <math|W<rsub|2>>
    (point splitting). This is what semiclassical gravity and cosmology
    need<nbsp><cite|Wal94>; examples are the Bunch\UDavies state of de Sitter
    space, used for inflationary perturbations, and the states of low energy
    on Friedmann\URobertson\UWalker spacetimes (adiabatic states of finite
    order are, in general, only approximately Hadamard). In the language of
    these lectures, Remark<nbsp><reference|rem:KL>(iii) and its Euclidean
    version (Remark<nbsp><hlink|3.7|cqft_notes_L2.tm#rem:KL-euclid>) show
    that in flat space every theory is at least as singular as the free
    field. The Hadamard condition prescribes exactly the free singularity,
    and the Wick ordering of Lecture 2 is the flat-space version of the
    subtraction of the Hadamard parametrix.
  </remark>

  <subsection|Why operator-valued distributions?><label|sec:distrib>

  <paragraph|The free field.> Let <math|m\<gtr\>0>,
  <math|\<omega\><around|(|<math-bf|p>|)>=<around|(|<around|\||<math-bf|p>|\|><rsup|2>+m<rsup|2>|)><rsup|1/2>>,
  and let <math|\<mathd\>\<Omega\><rsub|m><around|(|<math-bf|p>|)>=\<mathd\><math-bf|p>/<around|(|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|s>*2*\<omega\><around|(|<math-bf|p>|)>|)>>
  be the Lorentz invariant measure on the mass hyperboloid
  <math|H<rsub|m><rsup|+>=<around|{|p:<space|0.17em>p<rsup|2>=m<rsup|2>,<space|0.17em>p<rsup|0>\<gtr\>0|}>>,
  parametrised by <math|<math-bf|p>>. On the Fock space over
  <math|\<frak-h\>=L<rsup|2>*<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|s>,\<mathd\>\<Omega\><rsub|m>|)>>
  let <math|a<around|(|<math-bf|p>|)>,a<rsup|\<ast\>><around|(|<math-bf|p>|)>>
  be the annihilation/creation operator-valued distributions,
  <math|<around|[|a<around|(|<math-bf|p>|)>,a<rsup|\<ast\>><around|(|<math-bf|q>|)>|]>=<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|s>*2*\<omega\><around|(|<math-bf|p>|)>*\<delta\>*<around|(|<math-bf|p>-<math-bf|q>|)>>,
  <math|a<around|(|<math-bf|p>|)>*\<Omega\>=0>. The free field is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:free-field>\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>=<big|int>\<mathd\>\<Omega\><rsub|m><around|(|<math-bf|p>|)>*<space|0.17em><around*|[|a<around|(|<math-bf|p>|)>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>p\<cdot\>x>+a<rsup|\<ast\>><around|(|<math-bf|p>|)>\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>p\<cdot\>x>|]><rsub|p<rsup|0>=\<omega\><around|(|<math-bf|p>|)>>.
  </equation>

  For <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>|)>> one gets
  <math|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>=a<rsup|\<ast\>><around|(|<wide|f|~>|)>*\<Omega\>>
  with <math|<wide|f|~><around|(|<math-bf|p>|)>=<big|int>f<around|(|x|)>\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>p\<cdot\>x>\<mathd\>x\|<rsub|p<rsup|0>=\<omega\><around|(|<math-bf|p>|)>>>,
  and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:W2>W<rsub|2>*<around|(|x-y|)>=<big|int>\<mathd\>\<Omega\><rsub|m><around|(|<math-bf|p>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>p\<cdot\><around|(|x-y|)>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<big|int>\<mathd\>\<Omega\><rsub|m><around|(|<math-bf|p>|)><space|0.17em><around|\||<wide|f|~><around|(|<math-bf|p>|)>|\|><rsup|2>\<less\>\<infty\>.
  </equation>

  But formally <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>*\<Omega\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=W<rsub|2><around|(|0|)>=<big|int>\<mathd\>\<Omega\><rsub|m>=+\<infty\>>
  for <math|s\<geq\>1>: field fluctuations at a point are infinite. The
  divergence is ultraviolet (large momenta). It is logarithmic for <math|s=1>
  and power-like for <math|s\<geq\>2>. This is not an artefact of the free
  field.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|No fields at a point; Wightman<nbsp><cite|Wig64>><label|prop:nopoint>
    Assume (W0)\U(W2) and suppose there is <math|\<psi\>\<in\>\<cal-H\>> such
    that <math|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>=<big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*U*<around|(|x,<with|math-font|Bbb|1>|)>*\<psi\><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>x>
    for all <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>|)>>, i.e.
    \P<math|\<varphi\><around|(|0|)>*\<Omega\>=\<psi\>> exists\Q. Then
    <math|\<psi\>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>*\<Omega\>>,
    hence <math|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,\<psi\>|\<rangle\>><around*|(|<big|int>f|)>*\<Omega\>>.
  </proposition>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lem:finite-inv>Let <math|d\<geq\>2> and let <math|\<mu\>> be a
    finite positive Borel measure on <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|<space|-0.17em>+>>
    invariant under <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|+>>. Then
    <math|\<mu\>=\<mu\><around|(|<around|{|0|}>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|0>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    A boost of rapidity <math|\<theta\>> in the <math|x<rsup|1>> direction
    acts as <math|p<rsup|0>\<pm\>p<rsup|1>\<mapsto\>\<mathe\><rsup|\<pm\>\<theta\>><around|(|p<rsup|0>\<pm\>p<rsup|1>|)>>,
    leaving the other components unchanged. Let
    <math|S<rsup|\<pm\>><rsub|j>=<around|{|p\<in\><wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|<space|-0.17em>+>:<space|0.17em>2<rsup|j>\<leq\>p<rsup|0>\<pm\>p<rsup|1>\<less\>2<rsup|j+1>|}>>,
    <math|j\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>>. The boost with
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|\<theta\>>=2> maps <math|S<rsup|+><rsub|j>> onto
    <math|S<rsup|+><rsub|j+1>>, so <math|\<mu\><around|(|S<rsup|+><rsub|j>|)>>
    is independent of <math|j>. These sets are disjoint and <math|\<mu\>> is
    finite, so <math|\<mu\><around|(|S<rsup|+><rsub|j>|)>=0>. The same
    argument applies to <math|S<rsup|-><rsub|j>>. Finally
    <math|<big|cup><rsub|j><around|(|S<rsup|+><rsub|j>\<cup\>S<rsup|-><rsub|j>|)>=<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|<space|-0.17em>+>\<setminus\><around|{|0|}>>,
    since <math|p<rsup|0>\<pm\>p<rsup|1>\<geq\>0> on
    <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|<space|-0.17em>+>> and
    <math|p<rsup|0>+p<rsup|1>=p<rsup|0>-p<rsup|1>=0> forces <math|p=0>.
  </proof>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:nopoint>>
    By covariance, <math|U*<around|(|0,\<Lambda\>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>=\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|<around|(|0,\<Lambda\>|)>>|)>*\<Omega\>>.
    Since <math|U*<around|(|0,\<Lambda\>|)>*U*<around|(|x,<with|math-font|Bbb|1>|)>=U*<around|(|\<Lambda\>*x,<with|math-font|Bbb|1>|)>*U*<around|(|0,\<Lambda\>|)>>,
    after the change of variables <math|x\<mapsto\>\<Lambda\>*x> this reads

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>U*<around|(|\<Lambda\>*x,<with|math-font|Bbb|1>|)>*<around*|(|U*<around|(|0,\<Lambda\>|)>*\<psi\>-\<psi\>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>x=0*<space|2em><text|for
      all >f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Letting <math|f\<to\>\<delta\><rsub|0>> gives
    <math|U*<around|(|0,\<Lambda\>|)>*\<psi\>=\<psi\>>. Let <math|\<mu\>> be
    the spectral measure of <math|\<psi\>> for the translations,
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,U*<around|(|a,<with|math-font|Bbb|1>|)>*\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>=<big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>p\<cdot\>a>\<mu\><around|(|\<mathd\>p|)>>.
    It is finite with mass <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<psi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>,
    supported in <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|<space|-0.17em>+>> by the
    spectrum condition, and Lorentz invariant because <math|\<psi\>> is. By
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:finite-inv>,
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,U*<around|(|a,<with|math-font|Bbb|1>|)>*\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<\|\|\>|\<psi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>
    for all <math|a>, hence <math|U*<around|(|a,<with|math-font|Bbb|1>|)>*\<psi\>=\<psi\>>
    (equality in Cauchy\USchwarz), and <math|\<psi\>\<in\>\<bbb-C\>*\<Omega\>>
    by uniqueness of the vacuum.
  </render-proof>

  <\remark>
    So the only theories with point-like fields have
    <math|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>\<propto\>\<Omega\>>; using
    locality and the Reeh\USchlieder theorem one then shows that
    <math|\<varphi\>> is a multiple of the identity<nbsp><cite|SW64|Wig64>.
    Physically (Bohr\URosenfeld), measuring a field at a sharp point requires
    infinite energy. Quantum fields are therefore <em|operator-valued
    distributions>, and every nonlinear expression in
    <math|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>> (interaction terms, field equations,
    composite operators) requires a definition. This is the root of
    renormalisation.
  </remark>

  <subsection|The two-point function: the Källén\ULehmann
  representation><label|sec:KL>

  The free two-point function <eqref|eq:W2> is the Fourier transform of the
  invariant measure on the mass shell. In every Wightman theory the two-point
  function is a positive superposition of such free ones. For
  <math|M\<geq\>0> let <math|W<rsub|2><rsup|<around|(|M|)>>> be the two-point
  function <eqref|eq:W2> of the free field of mass <math|M>. For <math|M=0>
  and <math|d\<geq\>3> it is defined with the invariant measure
  <math|\<mathd\>\<Omega\><rsub|0>=\<mathd\><math-bf|p>/<around|(|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|s>*2<around|\||<math-bf|p>|\|>|)>>
  on the boundary of the cone; for <math|d=2> the measure
  <math|\<mathd\>p/<around|\||p|\|>> is not locally finite at <math|p=0>, and
  <math|W<rsub|2><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>> is not defined. By translation
  invariance of <math|\<Omega\>>, <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=v*<big|int>f>
  for some <math|v\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Källén\ULehmann<nbsp><cite|Kal52|Leh54>><label|thm:KL> Assume
    (W0)\U(W2). There is a positive Borel measure <math|\<rho\>> on
    <math|<around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>>, the <em|spectral measure> of the field,
    such that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:KL>W<rsub|2>*<around|(|x-y|)>=v<rsup|2>+<big|int><rsub|<around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>>\<rho\><around|(|\<mathd\>M<rsup|2>|)>*<space|0.17em>W<rsub|2><rsup|<around|(|M|)>>*<around|(|x-y|)>
    </equation>

    in the sense of tempered distributions. The measure <math|\<rho\>> grows
    at most polynomially and is such that the right-hand side is tempered; in
    particular <math|\<rho\><around|(|<around|{|0|}>|)>=0> if <math|d=2>.
  </theorem>

  <\render-proof|Sketch>
    Let <math|\<psi\><rsub|f>\<assign\>\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>-v<around|(|<big|int>f|)>*\<Omega\>\<perp\>\<Omega\>>.
    By hermiticity <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\><rsub|<wide|f|\<bar\>>>,\<psi\><rsub|g>|\<rangle\>>=W<rsub|2>*<around|(|f\<otimes\>g|)>-v<rsup|2>*<big|int>f*<big|int>g>,
    and by covariance <math|U*<around|(|a,<with|math-font|Bbb|1>|)>*\<psi\><rsub|f>=\<psi\><rsub|f<rsub|<around|(|a,<with|math-font|Bbb|1>|)>>>>.
    Hence the truncated function <math|W<rsub|2><rsup|T>\<assign\>W<rsub|2>-v<rsup|2>>
    is a distribution of positive type in <math|x-y>. By the
    Bochner\USchwartz theorem (equivalently, the spectral theorem for the
    translations restricted to the closed span of the <math|\<psi\><rsub|f>>)
    it is the Fourier transform of a positive tempered measure <math|\<mu\>>
    on momentum space. The spectrum condition gives
    <math|<math-up|supp>\<mu\>\<subset\><wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|<space|-0.17em>+>>.
    Since <math|\<psi\><rsub|f>\<perp\>\<Omega\>> and <math|\<Omega\>> is the
    only translation invariant vector, <math|\<mu\><around|(|<around|{|0|}>|)>=0>.
    Covariance and <math|U*<around|(|0,\<Lambda\>|)>*\<Omega\>=\<Omega\>>
    make <math|\<mu\>> invariant under <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|+>>.
    The <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|+>>-orbits in
    <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|<space|-0.17em>+>\<setminus\><around|{|0|}>>
    are the hyperboloids <math|H<rsup|+><rsub|M>>, <math|M\<gtr\>0>, and (for
    <math|d\<geq\>3>) the punctured boundary of the cone, <math|M=0>. Each
    carries a unique invariant measure up to normalisation, namely
    <math|\<mathd\>\<Omega\><rsub|M>>. (For <math|d=2> the two half-lines
    <math|p<rsup|0>=\<pm\>p<rsup|1>\<gtr\>0> of the cone boundary are orbits
    too, but their invariant measures <math|\<mathd\>p<rsup|\<pm\>>/p<rsup|\<pm\>>>
    are not locally finite at <math|0>, so a tempered <math|\<mu\>> cannot
    charge them; hence <math|\<rho\><around|(|<around|{|0|}>|)>=0>.)
    Disintegrating <math|\<mu\>> along <math|M=<around|(|p<rsup|2>|)><rsup|1/2>>
    gives <math|\<mu\>=<big|int>\<rho\><around|(|\<mathd\>M<rsup|2>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<Omega\><rsub|M>>,
    which is <eqref|eq:KL>.
  </render-proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Reading the spectrum off <math|\<rho\>>><label|rem:KL> (i)
    <em|Free fields.> The free field has <math|\<rho\>=\<delta\><rsub|m<rsup|2>>>.
    Conversely, if <math|\<rho\>=\<delta\><rsub|m<rsup|2>>> with
    <math|m\<gtr\>0> then <math|\<varphi\>-v> is a free field of mass
    <math|m> (Jost\USchroer theorem<nbsp><cite|SW64>; it uses the full
    Wightman axioms, in particular locality (W3)). So an interacting theory
    must have <math|\<rho\>\<neq\>\<delta\><rsub|m<rsup|2>>>; this is not
    sufficient, since generalised free fields have arbitrary <math|\<rho\>>
    and no interaction (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:gen-free>).

    (ii) <em|Particles.> An atom <math|Z*\<delta\><rsub|m<rsup|2>>> of
    <math|\<rho\>> isolated from the rest of its support, <math|Z\<gtr\>0>,
    signals a particle of mass <math|m>: an isolated eigenvalue <math|m> of
    the mass operator <math|<around|(|H<rsup|2>-<math-bf|P><rsup|2>|)><rsup|1/2>>.
    The remaining part of <math|\<rho\>> describes multi-particle states. In
    the absence of bound states it starts at <math|<around|(|2*m|)><rsup|2>>,
    or at <math|<around|(|3*m|)><rsup|2>> if the theory is invariant under
    <math|\<varphi\>\<mapsto\>-\<varphi\>>. For weakly coupled
    <math|P<around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsub|2>> models the isolated one-particle
    shell was established in<nbsp><cite|GJS74>.

    (iii) <em|No point fields, again.> Formally
    <math|W<rsub|2><rsup|T><around|(|0|)>=<big|int>\<rho\><around|(|\<mathd\>M<rsup|2>|)>*<big|int>\<mathd\>\<Omega\><rsub|M>=\<infty\>>
    for any <math|\<rho\>\<neq\>0> when <math|d\<geq\>2>. The singularity at
    coincident points of a nontrivial Wightman field is at least as strong as
    that of the free field. Remark<nbsp><hlink|3.7|cqft_notes_L2.tm#rem:KL-euclid>
    gives a precise Euclidean version of this statement.

    (iv) <em|Canonical fields and field-strength renormalisation.> If the
    sharp-time fields exist and satisfy the CCR <eqref|eq:CCR>, then
    <math|<big|int>\<rho\><around|(|\<mathd\>M<rsup|2>|)>=1>
    (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:sum-rule>). Hence
    <math|Z=\<rho\><around|(|<around|{|m<rsup|2>|}>|)>\<leq\>1>, with
    equality iff <math|\<rho\>=\<delta\><rsub|m<rsup|2>>>. The time-zero
    fields of <math|P<around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsub|2>> models satisfy the
    CCR<nbsp><cite|GJ87>. In <math|d=4>, perturbation theory gives
    <math|<big|int>\<rho\>=\<infty\>>, i.e. \P<math|Z=0>\Q: the renormalised
    field cannot be canonical, and must be rescaled by a divergent factor
    (wave-function renormalisation).
  </remark>

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<\script-output|tikz|default>
