  </script-output|<image|<tuple|<#>|ps>||||>><label|fig:KL>>|Typical
  spectral measure of an interacting field with mass gap
  (Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:KL> and Remark<nbsp><reference|rem:KL>): an
  isolated atom at the one-particle mass (<math|0\<less\>Z\<leq\>1> for a
  canonical field), and a continuum starting at
  <math|<around|(|2*m|)><rsup|2>>, or at <math|<around|(|3*m|)><rsup|2>> in
  the presence of the symmetry <math|\<varphi\>\<mapsto\>-\<varphi\>>. The
  free field has <math|\<rho\>=\<delta\><rsub|m<rsup|2>>>.>

  <subsection|The quantum wave equation><label|sec:qwe>

  Since <math|<around|(|-p<rsup|2>+m<rsup|2>|)>\<mathe\><rsup|\<pm\>\<mathi\>p\<cdot\>x>=0>
  on the mass shell, the free field <eqref|eq:free-field> solves the
  Klein\UGordon equation in the sense of distributions:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:KG><around|(|\<Box\>+m<rsup|2>|)>*\<varphi\>=0,<space|2em>\<Box\>=\<partial\><rsub|t><rsup|2>-\<Delta\><rsub|<math-bf|x>>.
  </equation>

  Moreover, the <em|sharp-time fields> <math|\<varphi\>*<around|(|t,g|)>=<big|int>\<varphi\>*<around|(|t,<math-bf|x>|)>*g<around|(|<math-bf|x>|)>\<mathd\><math-bf|x>>
  and <math|\<pi\>*<around|(|t,g|)>>, with
  <math|\<pi\>=\<partial\><rsub|t>*\<varphi\>>, are well defined for
  <math|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|s>|)>>
  (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:restrictions>), and satisfy the canonical
  commutation relations (CCR)

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:CCR><around|[|\<varphi\>*<around|(|t,<math-bf|x>|)>,\<pi\>*<around|(|t,<math-bf|y>|)>|]>=\<mathi\>\<delta\>*<around|(|<math-bf|x>-<math-bf|y>|)>,<space|2em><around|[|\<varphi\>*<around|(|t,<math-bf|x>|)>,\<varphi\>*<around|(|t,<math-bf|y>|)>|]>=<around|[|\<pi\>*<around|(|t,<math-bf|x>|)>,\<pi\>*<around|(|t,<math-bf|y>|)>|]>=0.
  </equation>

  One may think of <math|\<varphi\>> as the solution of a <em|linear wave
  equation with quantum initial data>: <math|(\<varphi\>*<around|(|0,\<cdot\>|)>,\<pi\>(0,\<cdot\><around|)||)>>
  satisfy the CCR and are \Pdistributed\Q according to the vacuum.

  <paragraph|Free field on a spatial circle.> For <math|s=1> and space
  <math|\<bbb-T\><rsub|L>=\<bbb-R\>/L*\<bbb-Z\>>, the momenta are
  <math|k\<in\><frac|2*\<pi\>|L>*\<bbb-Z\>>,
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|k>=<around|(|k<rsup|2>+m<rsup|2>|)><rsup|1/2>>, and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:modes>\<varphi\>*<around|(|0,x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|k><around|(|2*L*\<omega\><rsub|k>|)><rsup|-1/2>*<around*|(|a<rsub|k>\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>k*x>+a<rsub|k><rsup|\<ast\>>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>k*x>|)>,<space|2em><around|[|a<rsub|k>,a<rsup|\<ast\>><rsub|k<rprime|'>>|]>=\<delta\><rsub|k*k<rprime|'>>,<space|2em>H<rsub|0>=<big|sum><rsub|k>\<omega\><rsub|k>*a<rsub|k><rsup|\<ast\>>*a<rsub|k>.
  </equation>

  The free field is an infinite collection of independent harmonic
  oscillators, one per mode. With an ultraviolet cutoff
  <math|<around|\||k|\|>\<leq\>R>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:logdiv><around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,\<varphi\><rsub|R>*<around|(|0,x|)><rsup|2>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=<big|sum><rsub|<around|\||k|\|>\<leq\>R><frac|1|2*L*\<omega\><rsub|k>>=<frac|1|2*\<pi\>>*log
    <frac|R|m>+O<around|(|1|)><long-arrow|\<rubber-rightarrow\>|R\<to\>\<infty\>|>\<infty\>.
  </equation>

  <paragraph|Interactions.> The classical nonlinear Klein\UGordon equation
  <math|<around|(|\<Box\>+m<rsup|2>|)>*u+\<lambda\>*u<rsup|3>=0> is
  Hamiltonian with <math|H=<big|int><around|[|<frac|1|2>*\<pi\><rsup|2>+<frac|1|2>\|\<nabla\>*u\|<rsup|2>+<frac|1|2>*m<rsup|2>*u<rsup|2>+<frac|\<lambda\>|4>*u<rsup|4>|]>\<mathd\><math-bf|x>>.
  Its quantum version would be the operator equation

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:NLKG><around|(|\<Box\>+m<rsup|2>|)>*\<varphi\>+\<lambda\>*\<varphi\><rsup|3>=0,
  </equation>

  together with the CCR <eqref|eq:CCR>. This immediately runs into three
  problems.

  <\enumerate-roman>
    <item><math|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)><rsup|3>> has no meaning, being the
    cube of a distribution. It must be <em|renormalised>. In <math|d=2> Wick
    ordering <math|:\<varphi\><rsup|3>>: suffices (cf. <eqref|eq:logdiv> and
    Section<nbsp><hlink|3.3|cqft_notes_L2.tm#sec:wick>); in <math|d=3> a
    further mass counterterm is needed in the equation (and a vacuum-energy
    counterterm in the Hamiltonian); in <math|d=4> the theory is expected to
    be trivial, as is proved for the scaling limits of critical lattice
    <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|4>> models<nbsp><cite|ADC21>.

    <item><em|Haag's theorem><nbsp><cite|Haa96>: in infinite volume the
    interacting field at fixed time cannot be unitarily equivalent to the
    free one, so there is no interaction picture. The interacting theory
    lives in a representation of the CCR inequivalent to the Fock one.

    <item>Even in finite volume, <math|H=H<rsub|0>+\<lambda\>*<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-T\><rsub|L>>:\<varphi\>*<around|(|0,x|)><rsup|4>:\<mathd\>x>
    is the sum of a positive operator and an operator unbounded below. That
    it is bounded below was Nelson's breakthrough<nbsp><cite|Nel66>, based on
    hypercontractivity. Its self-adjointness and the construction of the
    corresponding local Heisenberg dynamics, with finite propagation speed,
    are due to Glimm\UJaffe (<cite|GJ68> and its sequels) and
    Segal<nbsp><cite|Seg70>; see<nbsp><cite|BSZ92> for a textbook account of
    this Hamiltonian approach. Removing the volume cutoff for
    <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>> was achieved by Glimm and Jaffe in a
    series of difficult papers; the full set of Wightman axioms for weakly
    coupled <math|P<around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsub|2>> models was established
    in<nbsp><cite|GJS74>, already with Euclidean methods. For
    <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> see<nbsp><cite|GJ73|FO76>.
  </enumerate-roman>

  The Euclidean approach (Symanzik, Nelson, Osterwalder\USchrader,
  Guerra\URosen\USimon) simplified this enormously. We now explain how it
  arises.

  <subsection*|Exercises>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|The free two-point function><label|ex:W2> (a) Show that
    <math|W<rsub|2>> in <eqref|eq:W2> is a Lorentz invariant tempered
    distribution solving <math|<around|(|\<Box\>+m<rsup|2>|)>*W<rsub|2>=0>,
    whose Fourier transform <math|<big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>p\<cdot\>x>W<rsub|2><around|(|x|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>x>
    (Minkowski product) is <math|2*\<pi\>*<space|0.17em>\<theta\><around|(|p<rsup|0>|)>*\<delta\>*<around|(|p<rsup|2>-m<rsup|2>|)>>.
    (b) For <math|d=2> show that <math|W<rsub|2><around|(|0,x|)>=<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-1>*K<rsub|0><around|(|m<around|\||x|\|>|)>>
    and deduce the logarithmic singularity at <math|x=0>. (c) Show that
    <math|<around|[|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>,\<varphi\><around|(|0|)>|]>=W<rsub|2><around|(|x|)>-W<rsub|2>*<around|(|-x|)>>
    vanishes for spacelike <math|x>, i.e. the free field satisfies (W3).
    <em|Hint:> boost <math|x> to the plane <math|x<rsup|0>=0> and use the
    symmetry <math|<math-bf|p>\<mapsto\>-<math-bf|p>> of
    <math|\<mathd\>\<Omega\><rsub|m>>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Invariant measures on the hyperboloid>Show that
    <math|<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>\<theta\><around|(|p<rsup|0>|)>*\<delta\>*<around|(|p<rsup|2>-m<rsup|2>|)>*g<around|(|p|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>p=<big|int>g<around|(|\<omega\><around|(|<math-bf|p>|)>,<math-bf|p>|)><space|0.17em><frac|\<mathd\><math-bf|p>|2*\<omega\><around|(|<math-bf|p>|)>>>,
    that this is the unique <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|+>>-invariant
    measure on <math|H<rsup|+><rsub|m>> up to constants, and give a direct
    proof that <math|H<rsub|m><rsup|+>> carries no finite invariant measure.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Which restrictions make sense?><label|ex:restrictions> For a
    distribution <math|T> on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>, the free field
    \P<math|\<varphi\><around|(|T|)>*\<Omega\>>\Q is defined iff
    <math|<big|int>\<mathd\>\<Omega\><rsub|m><around|\||<wide|T|~>|\|><rsup|2>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    Decide for which of the following this holds: (a)
    <math|T=\<delta\><rsub|x>>; (b) <math|T=\<delta\><around|(|t|)>\<otimes\>g<around|(|<math-bf|x>|)>>
    with <math|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|s>|)>> (sharp time);
    (c) <math|T=h<around|(|t|)>\<otimes\>\<delta\><around|(|<math-bf|x>|)>>
    with <math|h\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<bbb-R\>|)>>; (d)
    <math|T=\<delta\><rprime|'><around|(|t|)>\<otimes\>g<around|(|<math-bf|x>|)>>,
    which gives <math|-\<pi\>*<around|(|0,g|)>>. Verify the CCR
    <eqref|eq:CCR>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Analyticity from the spectrum condition><label|ex:analytic> (a)
    Let <math|H\<geq\>0> be self-adjoint. Show that
    <math|z\<mapsto\><around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>z*H>\<chi\>|\<rangle\>>>
    is bounded and analytic on <math|<around|{|<math-up|Im>z\<less\>0|}>>,
    continuous up to the boundary, and is determined by its values on the
    negative imaginary half-line. (b) Using (W0), show that
    <math|a\<mapsto\>U*<around|(|a,<with|math-font|Bbb|1>|)>*\<psi\>> extends
    to a bounded analytic <math|\<cal-H\>>-valued function on the tube
    <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>+\<mathi\>V<rsub|<space|-0.17em>+>>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|A canonical sum rule><label|ex:sum-rule> (a) For the free field of
    mass <math|M>, write <math|<around|[|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>,\<varphi\><around|(|0|)>|]>=W<rsub|2><rsup|<around|(|M|)>><around|(|x|)>-W<rsub|2><rsup|<around|(|M|)>>*<around|(|-x|)>>
    and show that <math|\<partial\><rsub|t><around|[|\<varphi\>*<around|(|t,<math-bf|x>|)>,\<varphi\>*<around|(|0,<with|math-font-family|bf|0>|)>|]>\|<rsub|t=0>=-\<mathi\>\<delta\><around|(|<math-bf|x>|)>>,
    independently of <math|M>. (b) Assume a Wightman field has sharp-time
    fields satisfying <eqref|eq:CCR>. Using <eqref|eq:KL>, show (at least
    formally) that <math|<big|int>\<rho\><around|(|\<mathd\>M<rsup|2>|)>=1>.
    Deduce <math|0\<leq\>Z\<leq\>1> in Remark<nbsp><reference|rem:KL>(iv).
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Generalised free fields><label|ex:gen-free> Let
    <math|\<varphi\><rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,\<varphi\><rsub|n>> be independent
    free fields of distinct masses <math|M<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,M<rsub|n>\<gtr\>0>,
    on the tensor product of their Fock spaces, and
    <math|\<varphi\>=<big|sum><rsub|i>c<rsub|i>*\<varphi\><rsub|i>> with
    <math|c<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<setminus\><around|{|0|}>>. (a) Show that
    <math|\<varphi\>> satisfies (W0)\U(W4) and has
    <math|\<rho\>=<big|sum><rsub|i>c<rsub|i><rsup|2>*\<delta\><rsub|M<rsub|i><rsup|2>>>.
    (b) Show that <math|<big|prod><rsub|i><around|(|\<Box\>+M<rsub|i><rsup|2>|)>*\<varphi\>=0>,
    but that <math|\<varphi\>> satisfies no Klein\UGordon equation of a
    single mass if <math|n\<geq\>2>. (c) Show that all truncated Wightman
    functions of order <math|\<geq\>3> vanish, so there is no scattering. (d)
    Extend the construction to an arbitrary polynomially bounded
    <math|\<rho\>> (with <math|\<rho\><around|(|<around|{|0|}>|)>=0> if
    <math|d=2>).
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Wick ordering on the circle>Using <eqref|eq:modes> and the time
    evolution <math|a<rsub|k>\<mapsto\>a<rsub|k>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>\<omega\><rsub|k>*t>>,
    verify <eqref|eq:KG>, <eqref|eq:CCR> and <eqref|eq:logdiv>. For
    <math|g\<in\>C<rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|\<bbb-T\><rsub|L>|)>> with
    <math|<big|int>g\<neq\>0> show that <math|<big|int>g<around|(|x|)>*\<varphi\><rsub|R>*<around|(|0,x|)><rsup|2>\<mathd\>x*<space|0.17em>\<Omega\>>
    diverges as <math|R\<to\>\<infty\>>, while
    <math|<big|int>g<around|(|x|)>:\<varphi\><rsub|R>*<around|(|0,x|)><rsup|2>:\<mathd\>x*<space|0.17em>\<Omega\>>
    converges in Fock space, where <math|:\<varphi\><rsub|R><rsup|2>:\<assign\>\<varphi\><rsub|R><rsup|2>-<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,\<varphi\><rsub|R><rsup|2>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>>.
  </exercise>

  <section|From real to imaginary time><label|sec:euclid>

  We work with a regularised version of <eqref|eq:NLKG> with finitely many
  degrees of freedom, where everything is rigorous, and follow how the
  quantum evolution turns into a classical probability measure.

  <subsection|A lattice Hamiltonian on the circle>

  Fix <math|L\<gtr\>0>, <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|\<varepsilon\>=L/N>,
  and the periodic lattice <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=\<varepsilon\>*\<bbb-Z\>/L*\<bbb-Z\>\<subset\>\<bbb-T\><rsub|L>>
  (<math|N> sites). Configurations are <math|\<varphi\>=<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|x>|)><rsub|x\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|\<Lambda\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>>.
  In the Schrödinger representation <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=L<rsup|2>*<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|\<Lambda\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>,\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>>,
  <math|\<varphi\><rsub|x>> acts by multiplication and
  <math|\<pi\><rsub|x>\<assign\>-\<mathi\>\<varepsilon\><rsup|-1>*\<partial\>/\<partial\>*\<varphi\><rsub|x>>,
  so that <math|<around|[|\<varphi\><rsub|x>,\<pi\><rsub|y>|]>=\<mathi\>\<varepsilon\><rsup|-1>*<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|x=y>>,
  the lattice version of <math|\<mathi\>\<delta\>*<around|(|x-y|)>>. Let
  <math|V> be a polynomial bounded below (e.g.

  <math|V<around|(|u|)>=\<lambda\>*u<rsup|4>>) and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:Hlat>H<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<assign\><big|sum><rsub|x\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>\<varepsilon\>*<around*|[|<tfrac|1|2>\<pi\><rsub|x><rsup|2>+<tfrac|1|2><around|(|\<nabla\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*\<varphi\>|)><rsub|x><rsup|2>+<tfrac|1|2>m<rsup|2>*\<varphi\><rsub|x><rsup|2>+V<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|x>|)>|]>=-<frac|1|2*\<varepsilon\>>*\<Delta\><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|\<Lambda\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>>+U<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>,
  </equation>

  where <math|<around|(|\<nabla\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*\<varphi\>|)><rsub|x>=<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|x+\<varepsilon\>>-\<varphi\><rsub|x>|)>/\<varepsilon\>>
  and <math|U<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=\<varepsilon\>*<big|sum><rsub|x><around|[|<frac|1|2>*<around|(|\<nabla\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*\<varphi\>|)><rsub|x><rsup|2>+<frac|1|2>*m<rsup|2>*\<varphi\><rsub|x><rsup|2>+V<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|x>|)>|]>>.
  A short computation (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:heisenberg>) shows that
  the Heisenberg fields <math|\<varphi\><rsub|x><around|(|t|)>=\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>t*H<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>\<varphi\><rsub|x>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>t*H<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>>
  satisfy

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:latticewave>\<partial\><rsub|t><rsup|2>*\<varphi\><rsub|x><around|(|t|)>=<around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*\<varphi\><around|(|t|)>|)><rsub|x>-m<rsup|2>*\<varphi\><rsub|x><around|(|t|)>-V<rprime|'><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|x><around|(|t|)>|)>,<space|2em><around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*\<varphi\>|)><rsub|x>=<frac|\<varphi\><rsub|x+\<varepsilon\>>+\<varphi\><rsub|x-\<varepsilon\>>-2*\<varphi\><rsub|x>|\<varepsilon\><rsup|2>>,
  </equation>

  a lattice regularisation of the quantum wave equation <eqref|eq:NLKG>, now
  with a perfectly rigorous meaning.

  <math|H<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> is a Schrödinger operator on
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|N>> with a confining potential
  (<math|U<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<geq\>c\|\<varphi\>\|<rsup|2>-C> since
  <math|m\<gtr\>0>). Hence it is essentially self-adjoint on
  <math|C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|c>>, bounded below, with compact resolvent,
  and <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-t*H<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>> is trace class for
  <math|t\<gtr\>0>. The integral kernel of
  <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-t*H<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>> is strictly positive, so
  by the Perron\UFrobenius argument the ground state energy
  <math|E<rsub|0>=inf <math-up|spec>H<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> is a simple
  eigenvalue with a strictly positive eigenfunction
  <math|\<Omega\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>, the <em|lattice
  vacuum><nbsp><cite|Sim79>. We set

  <\equation>
    <wide|H|\<bar\>>\<assign\>H<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>-E<rsub|0>\<geq\>0<space|2em><around|(|<text|positivity
    of the energy>|)>.
  </equation>

  <subsection|Positivity of the energy and analytic continuation>

  Let <math|F<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,F<rsub|n>:\<bbb-R\><rsup|\<Lambda\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>\<to\>\<bbb-C\>>
  be bounded measurable (acting as multiplication operators) and
  <math|F<rsub|j><around|(|t|)>=\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>t*H<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>F<rsub|j>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>t*H<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>>.
  Since <math|<wide|H|\<bar\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=0>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:Wlat>W<around|(|t<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,t<rsub|n>|)>\<assign\><around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,F<rsub|1><around|(|t<rsub|1>|)>*\<cdots\>*F<rsub|n><around|(|t<rsub|n>|)>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,F<rsub|1>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\><around|(|t<rsub|1>-t<rsub|2>|)>*<wide|H|\<bar\>>>F<rsub|2>*\<cdots\>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\><around|(|t<rsub|n-1>-t<rsub|n>|)>*<wide|H|\<bar\>>>F<rsub|n>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Wick rotation><label|prop:wick-rot> <math|W> depends only on
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|j>=t<rsub|j>-t<rsub|j+1>> and extends to a function
    <math|\<cal-W\><around|(|\<zeta\><rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,\<zeta\><rsub|n-1>|)>>
    which is bounded and continuous on <math|<wide|\<bbb-C\>|\<bar\>><rsub|-><rsup|<space|0.17em>n-1>>
    and analytic on <math|\<bbb-C\><rsub|-><rsup|n-1>>,
    <math|\<bbb-C\><rsub|->=<around|{|<math-up|Im>\<zeta\>\<less\>0|}>>. At
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|j>=-\<mathi\><around|(|\<tau\><rsub|j>-\<tau\><rsub|j+1>|)>>,
    <math|\<tau\><rsub|1>\<geq\>\<ldots\>\<geq\>\<tau\><rsub|n>>, it gives
    the <em|Schwinger functions>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:schwinger>S<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,\<tau\><rsub|n>|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,F<rsub|1>\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|1>-\<tau\><rsub|2>|)>*<wide|H|\<bar\>>>F<rsub|2>*\<cdots\>\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|n-1>-\<tau\><rsub|n>|)>*<wide|H|\<bar\>>>F<rsub|n>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|\<rangle\>>,
    </equation>

    and the Schwinger functions determine the Wightman functions <math|W>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    By the spectral theorem, <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>\<zeta\>*<wide|H|\<bar\>>>=<big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>\<zeta\>*\<lambda\>>\<mathd\>E<around|(|\<lambda\>|)>>
    with <math|<around|\||\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>\<zeta\>*\<lambda\>>|\|>=\<mathe\><rsup|\<lambda\><math-up|Im>\<zeta\>>\<leq\>1>
    for <math|\<lambda\>\<geq\>0>, <math|<math-up|Im>\<zeta\>\<leq\>0>. It is
    therefore a contraction, strongly continuous on
    <math|<wide|\<bbb-C\>|\<bar\>><rsub|->> and analytic in the interior.
    Products of such operators give separately analytic, bounded, continuous
    functions, hence jointly analytic ones. If <math|\<cal-W\>> vanishes on
    <math|<around|(|-\<mathi\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|+>|)><rsup|n-1>>, the identity
    theorem in <math|\<zeta\><rsub|1>> (with the other variables on the
    imaginary axis) shows that it vanishes for all
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|1>\<in\>\<bbb-C\><rsub|->>. Proceeding variable by
    variable, and using continuity up to the boundary, gives
    <math|W\<equiv\>0>.
  </proof>

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<\script-output|tikz|default>
    \\documentclass[tikz]{standalone}

    \\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}

    \\usepackage[sc]{mathpazo}

    \\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,mathtools,bm}

    \\usepackage[mathscr]{eucal}

    \\usetikzlibrary{decorations.pathreplacing,arrows.meta,positioning}

    \\usepackage[dvipsnames]{xcolor}

    \;

    \\begin{document}

    \\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85,\>=Stealth]

    \ \ \\fill[MidnightBlue!10] (-2,0) rectangle (2,-2);

    \ \ \\draw[-\>] (-2.2,0) -- (2.3,0) node[right] {\\small
    $\\operatorname{Re}\\zeta$};

    \ \ \\draw[-\>] (0,-2.2) -- (0,1.2) node[above] {\\small
    $\\operatorname{Im}\\zeta$};

    \ \ \\draw[very thick,MidnightBlue] (0.15,0) -- (1.9,0);

    \ \ \\node[MidnightBlue,above] at (1.1,0.05) {\\small real time};

    \ \ \\draw[very thick,BrickRed] (0,-0.15) -- (0,-1.9);

    \ \ \\node[BrickRed,anchor=west] at (0.1,-1.85) {\\small
    $\\zeta=-\\mathrm{i}\\tau$};

    \ \ \\draw[-\>,thick,gray] (1.3,-0.1) arc (0:-80:1.3);

    \ \ \\node[MidnightBlue] at (-1.15,-1.6) {\\small $\\bar H\\geq0$};

    \\end{tikzpicture}

    \\end{document}
  </script-output|<image|<tuple|<#>|ps>||||>><label|fig:wick-rotation>>|Wick
  rotation (Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:wick-rot>). Since
  <math|<wide|H|\<bar\>>\<geq\>0>, <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>\<zeta\>*<wide|H|\<bar\>>>>
  is a contraction on the lower half-plane
  <math|<math-up|Im>\<zeta\>\<leq\>0>, analytic in its interior. The Wightman
  functions on the real axis and the Schwinger functions on the negative
  imaginary axis are boundary values and restrictions of the same analytic
  function.>

  <\remark>
    This is exactly where positivity of the energy enters: it allows rotating
    <math|t\<mapsto\>-\<mathi\>\<tau\>> in all time differences
    simultaneously. It will be used twice more:
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-\<tau\>*<wide|H|\<bar\>>>>, <math|\<tau\>\<geq\>0>,
    is a contraction semigroup, which can be represented as a path integral
    (Feynman\UKac); and <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-T*<wide|H|\<bar\>>>> converges
    to the projection onto <math|ker <wide|H|\<bar\>>> as
    <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>>, i.e. onto the vacuum when it is unique
    (Remark<nbsp><reference|rem:clustering>). (That the Feynman\UKac weights
    are positive is a separate fact, coming from the kinetic term.) In a
    relativistic theory, the Wightman functions are boundary values of
    functions analytic in the forward tube
    (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:analytic>). By Lorentz covariance and
    locality (the Bargmann\UHall\UWightman theorem and Jost points) these
    extend to a domain containing all non-coincident Euclidean points, where
    they define symmetric, Euclidean invariant Schwinger
    functions<nbsp><cite|SW64|OS73>.
  </remark>

  <subsection|Discretising imaginary time: the Feynman\UKac formula>

  The kinetic part of <math|H<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> generates a Gaussian
  semigroup with kernel

  <\equation*>
    \<mathe\><rsup|<frac|\<delta\>|2*\<varepsilon\>>*\<Delta\>><around|(|\<varphi\>,\<varphi\><rprime|'>|)>=<around*|(|<frac|2*\<pi\>*\<delta\>|\<varepsilon\>>|)><rsup|-N/2>*exp
    <around*|(|-<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2*\<delta\>>*<around|\||\<varphi\>-\<varphi\><rprime|'>|\|><rsup|2>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Hence the symmetric Trotter approximant
  <math|T<rsub|\<delta\>>\<assign\>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<delta\>*U<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>/2><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|<frac|\<delta\>|2*\<varepsilon\>>*\<Delta\>><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<delta\>*U<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>/2>>
  has the explicit positive kernel

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:transfer>T<rsub|\<delta\>>*<around|(|\<varphi\>,\<varphi\><rprime|'>|)>=<around*|(|<frac|2*\<pi\>*\<delta\>|\<varepsilon\>>|)><rsup|-N/2>*exp
    <around*|(|-<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2*\<delta\>>*<big|sum><rsub|x><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|x>-\<varphi\><rprime|'><rsub|x>|)><rsup|2>-<frac|\<delta\>|2>*<around*|(|U<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>+U<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|\<varphi\><rprime|'>|)>|)>|)>,
  </equation>

  and <math|T<rsub|\<delta\>><rsup|<around|\<lfloor\>|\<tau\>/\<delta\>|\<rfloor\>>>\<to\>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<tau\>*H<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>>
  strongly as <math|\<delta\>\<to\>0> (Trotter product
  formula<nbsp><cite|RS80|Sim79>). Let <math|T=J*\<delta\>> and consider the
  space-time lattice

  <\equation>
    \<Lambda\>=\<Lambda\><rsub|\<delta\>,\<varepsilon\>><rsup|T,L>\<assign\><around|(|\<delta\>*\<bbb-Z\>/T*\<bbb-Z\>|)>\<times\><around|(|\<varepsilon\>*\<bbb-Z\>/L*\<bbb-Z\>|)>,<space|2em>\<varphi\>=<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<tau\>,x>|)><rsub|<around|(|\<tau\>,x|)>\<in\>\<Lambda\>>,<space|1em>\<varphi\><rsub|\<tau\>>\<assign\><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<tau\>,x>|)><rsub|x\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>.
  </equation>

  Writing the trace of a product of integral operators as an integral over
  closed chains, we get <math|<math-up|Tr><around|(|T<rsub|\<delta\>><rsup|J>|)>=<around|(|2*\<pi\>*\<delta\>/\<varepsilon\>|)><rsup|-N*J/2>*<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|\<Lambda\>>>\<mathe\><rsup|-S<rsub|\<delta\>,\<varepsilon\>><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>><big|prod><rsub|u\<in\>\<Lambda\>>\<mathd\>\<varphi\><rsub|u>>
  with the <em|lattice Euclidean action>

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:lattice-action>S<rsub|\<delta\>,\<varepsilon\>><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|\<tau\>,x|)>\<in\>\<Lambda\>>\<varepsilon\>*\<delta\>*<around*|[|<tfrac|1|2><around*|(|<frac|\<varphi\><rsub|\<tau\>+\<delta\>,x>-\<varphi\><rsub|\<tau\>,x>|\<delta\>>|)><rsup|2>+<tfrac|1|2><around*|(|<frac|\<varphi\><rsub|\<tau\>,x+\<varepsilon\>>-\<varphi\><rsub|\<tau\>,x>|\<varepsilon\>>|)><rsup|2>+<tfrac|1|2>m<rsup|2>*\<varphi\><rsub|\<tau\>,x><rsup|2>+V<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<tau\>,x>|)>|]>.
  </equation>

  This discretises <math|S<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<big|iint><rsub|\<bbb-T\><rsub|T>\<times\>\<bbb-T\><rsub|L>><around|[|<frac|1|2>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|\<tau\>>*\<varphi\>|)><rsup|2>+<frac|1|2>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|x>*\<varphi\>|)><rsup|2>+<frac|1|2>*m<rsup|2>*\<varphi\><rsup|2>+V<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>|]><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<tau\><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>x>.
  Imaginary time and space now play symmetric roles: the Minkowski metric has
  become Euclidean. Define the probability measure

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:lattice-measure>\<mu\><rsub|\<delta\>,\<varepsilon\>><rsup|T,L><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>\<assign\>Z<rsup|-1>\<mathe\><rsup|-S<rsub|\<delta\>,\<varepsilon\>><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>><big|prod><rsub|u\<in\>\<Lambda\>>\<mathd\>\<varphi\><rsub|u>.
  </equation>

  Inserting multiplication operators between the factors
  <math|T<rsub|\<delta\>>> gives, for <math|\<tau\><rsub|1>\<geq\>\<ldots\>\<geq\>\<tau\><rsub|n>>
  in <math|\<delta\>*\<bbb-Z\>> with <math|\<tau\><rsub|1>-\<tau\><rsub|n>\<less\>T>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:FK-lattice><frac|<math-up|Tr><around*|(|F<rsub|1>*T<rsub|\<delta\>><rsup|k<rsub|1>>*F<rsub|2>*T<rsub|\<delta\>><rsup|k<rsub|2>>*\<cdots\>*F<rsub|n>*T<rsub|\<delta\>><rsup|k<rsub|n>>|)>|<math-up|Tr><around|(|T<rsub|\<delta\>><rsup|J>|)>>=<big|int>F<rsub|1><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<tau\><rsub|1>>|)>*\<cdots\>*F<rsub|n><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<tau\><rsub|n>>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<mu\><rsub|\<delta\>,\<varepsilon\>><rsup|T,L><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>,
  </equation>

  where <math|k<rsub|j>=<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|j>-\<tau\><rsub|j+1>|)>/\<delta\>>
  for <math|j\<less\>n> and <math|k<rsub|n>=J-<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>n>k<rsub|j>>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Feynman\UKac\UNelson><label|prop:FK> For bounded continuous
    <math|F<rsub|j>> and dyadic <math|\<tau\><rsub|1>\<geq\>\<ldots\>\<geq\>\<tau\><rsub|n>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:FK><around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,F<rsub|1>\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|1>-\<tau\><rsub|2>|)>*<wide|H|\<bar\>>>F<rsub|2>*\<cdots\>*F<rsub|n>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|\<rangle\>>=lim<rsub|T\<to\>\<infty\>>
      lim<rsub|\<delta\>\<to\>0> <big|int>F<rsub|1><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<tau\><rsub|1>>|)>*\<cdots\>*F<rsub|n><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<tau\><rsub|n>>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<mu\><rsub|\<delta\>,\<varepsilon\>><rsup|T,L><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>.
    </equation>
  </proposition>

  <\render-proof|Sketch>
    The Gaussian factor in <eqref|eq:lattice-measure> gives precisely the
    finite-dimensional distributions, at times
    <math|\<tau\>\<in\>\<delta\>*\<bbb-Z\>/T*\<bbb-Z\>>, of the
    (unnormalised) Brownian loop measure on
    <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|\<Lambda\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>> of length <math|T>
    and generator <math|<frac|1|2*\<varepsilon\>>*\<Delta\>> (variance
    <math|t/\<varepsilon\>> per coordinate). The potential term
    <math|\<delta\>*<big|sum><rsub|\<tau\>>U<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<tau\>>|)>>
    is a Riemann sum for <math|<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|T>U<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|\<omega\><rsub|\<tau\>>|)>\<mathd\>\<tau\>>.
    Hence, as <math|\<delta\>\<to\>0> (along dyadic <math|\<delta\>>, say, so
    that the <math|\<tau\><rsub|j>> stay on the lattice), both sides of
    <eqref|eq:FK-lattice> converge to the Feynman\UKac expression
    <math|<math-up|Tr><around|(|F<rsub|1>\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|1>-\<tau\><rsub|2>|)>*H>\<cdots\>*F<rsub|n>\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|T-\<tau\><rsub|1>+\<tau\><rsub|n>|)>*H>|)>/<math-up|Tr><around|(|\<mathe\><rsup|-T*H>|)>>,
    written as an integral over Brownian loops weighted by <math|exp
    <around|(|-<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|T>U<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>><nbsp><cite|Sim79>.
    The limit <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>> uses that <math|E<rsub|0>> is simple
    and isolated (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:vacuum>).
  </render-proof>

  Vacuum expectations of the quantum system in imaginary time are thus
  <em|moments of a classical probability measure>. As <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>>
  the time-slices <math|<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<tau\>>|)><rsub|\<tau\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>
  form a stationary Markov process: the ground-state transform of
  <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-\<tau\>*<wide|H|\<bar\>>>>, whose one-time marginal
  is <math|\<Omega\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsup|2>\<mathd\>\<varphi\>>.
  For <math|N=1>, <math|V=0> this is the Ornstein\UUhlenbeck process
  (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:OU> and Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:ou>).

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|<tuple|<#>|ou_path.pdf>|0.95par|||><label|fig:ou>>|Left:
  a sample of the stationary Ornstein\UUhlenbeck process, the imaginary-time
  path of one harmonic oscillator, i.e. the GFF in <math|d=1>
  (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:OU>). Right: its empirical two-point function
  against the Schwinger function <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-m*\<tau\>>/2*m>.>

  <subsection|Classical statistical mechanics>

  Take the isotropic lattice <math|\<delta\>=\<varepsilon\>>. Then
  <eqref|eq:lattice-action> becomes

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:spin-system>S<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|<around|\<langle\>|u,v|\<rangle\>>><tfrac|1|2><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|u>-\<varphi\><rsub|v>|)><rsup|2>+\<varepsilon\><rsup|2>*<big|sum><rsub|u><around*|[|<tfrac|1|2>m<rsup|2>*\<varphi\><rsub|u><rsup|2>+V<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|u>|)>|]>,
  </equation>

  the first sum running over nearest-neighbour pairs of the two-dimensional
  periodic lattice <math|\<Lambda\>>. So <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<propto\><big|prod><rsub|<around|\<langle\>|u,v|\<rangle\>>>\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|1|2>*<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|u>-\<varphi\><rsub|v>|)><rsup|2>><big|prod><rsub|u>\<nu\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\><rsub|u>|)>>,
  with single-site measure <math|\<nu\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|\<mathd\>a|)>=\<mathe\><rsup|-\<varepsilon\><rsup|2>*<around|(|<frac|1|2>*m<rsup|2>*a<rsup|2>+V<around|(|a|)>|)>>\<mathd\>a>.
  This is a <em|ferromagnetic nearest-neighbour spin system> with continuous,
  unbounded spins, i.e. a short-range model of classical statistical
  mechanics. In spacetime dimension <math|d> one gets
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsup|d-2>*<big|sum><rsub|<around|\<langle\>|u,v|\<rangle\>>>>
  and <math|\<varepsilon\><rsup|d>*<big|sum><rsub|u>> instead
  (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:lattice-action>). The nearest-neighbour
  structure makes <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> <em|reflection positive>
  (Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:lattice> and
  Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:RP-lattice>), the Euclidean shadow of the
  positivity of <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-\<varepsilon\>*H<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>>.
  Conversely, reflection positivity is enough to reconstruct a Hilbert space
  and a positive Hamiltonian (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:toyOS>).

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<\script-output|tikz|default>
