  </script-output|<image|<tuple|<#>|ps>||||>><label|fig:lattice>>|The
  Euclidean space-time lattice. Rows are configurations of the quantum system
  at fixed imaginary time; the transfer matrix
  <math|T<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<approx\>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<varepsilon\>*H<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>>
  connects consecutive rows. The nearest-neighbour structure makes the
  measure reflection positive with respect to <math|\<Theta\>>.>

  <big-table|<with|par-mode|center|font-size|0.84|<tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|8|8|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|Quantum
  theory (real time)>|<cell|Euclidean field theory>|<cell|Statistical
  mechanics>>|<row|<cell|positive Hilbert space,
  <math|H\<geq\>0>>|<cell|reflection positivity>|<cell|RP of n.n.
  interactions>>|<row|<cell|<math|\<mathe\><rsup|-\<varepsilon\>*H>>>|<cell|time
  translation by <math|\<varepsilon\>>>|<cell|transfer
  matrix>>|<row|<cell|vacuum <math|\<Omega\>>>|<cell|infinite-volume measure
  (<math|T\<to\>\<infty\>>)>|<cell|infinite-volume Gibbs
  state>>|<row|<cell|inverse temperature <math|\<beta\>>>|<cell|length
  <math|T> of the time circle>|<cell|<emdash>>>|<row|<cell|mass gap
  <math|E<rsub|1>-E<rsub|0>>>|<cell|exponential decay of
  correlations>|<cell|inverse correlation length
  <math|1/\<xi\>>>>|<row|<cell|continuum limit>|<cell|removal of UV cutoff
  <math|\<varepsilon\>\<to\>0>>|<cell|critical point, scaling
  limit>>|<row|<cell|renormalisation>|<cell|counterterms in the
  action>|<cell|tuning parameters to criticality>>>>><label|tab:dictionary>>|A
  dictionary between the three pictures.>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Continuum limit <math|=> critical point>In lattice units the model
    <eqref|eq:spin-system> has mass <math|\<varepsilon\>*m\<to\>0>, so its
    correlation length is <math|\<xi\>\<approx\><around|(|\<varepsilon\>*m|)><rsup|-1>\<to\>\<infty\>>
    lattice spacings (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:corrlength>). The continuum
    limit of the regularised QFT is therefore the <em|scaling limit of a
    family of short-range spin systems approaching a critical point>.
    Conversely, any reflection positive, short-range critical model with a
    nontrivial scaling limit yields a candidate Euclidean QFT. This is
    Wilson's point of view on renormalisation<nbsp><cite|WK74>. For instance,
    the Griffiths\USimon construction realises <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4>>
    single-spin measures as limits of normalised sums of ferromagnetically
    coupled Ising spins<nbsp><cite|GS73>, and the critical 4D Ising and
    <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|4>> models have Gaussian scaling
    limits<nbsp><cite|ADC21>. See<nbsp><cite|GRS75> for the systematic use of
    lattice approximations for <math|P<around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsub|2>>.
  </remark>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Renormalisation already appears>For
    <math|V<around|(|a|)>=\<lambda\>*a<rsup|4>>, the limit
    <math|\<varepsilon\>\<to\>0> at fixed <math|<around|(|m,\<lambda\>|)>>
    does not produce an interacting theory: the Gaussian part alone has
    <math|c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<assign\>\<bbb-E\><around|[|\<varphi\><rsub|u><rsup|2>|]>=<frac|1|2*\<pi\>>*log
    <frac|1|\<varepsilon\>*m>+O<around|(|1|)>> (compare <eqref|eq:logdiv>).
    One must take <math|\<varepsilon\>>-dependent couplings, e.g.
    <math|V<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|a|)>=\<lambda\>*<around|(|a<rsup|4>-6*c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*a<rsup|2>|)>>
    (a divergent mass counterterm), i.e. <math|\<lambda\>> times the Wick
    power <math|:a<rsup|4>>: of Section<nbsp><hlink|3.3|cqft_notes_L2.tm#sec:wick>
    up to a constant. In <math|d=2> this is all that is needed; in <math|d=3>
    more counterterms are required.
  </remark>

  <subsection|Path integrals and the Osterwalder\USchrader axioms>

  Formally, as <math|\<varepsilon\>\<to\>0> and <math|L,T\<to\>\<infty\>>,
  <eqref|eq:lattice-measure> becomes the Euclidean path integral

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:path-integral>\<mu\><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>*<space|0.27em>`*`="*<space|0.27em>Z<rsup|-1>*exp
    <around*|(|-<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>><around*|[|<tfrac|1|2>\|\<nabla\>*\<varphi\>\|<rsup|2>+<tfrac|1|2>m<rsup|2>*\<varphi\><rsup|2>+V<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>|]>\<mathd\>x|)>*\<cal-D\>*\<varphi\>.
  </equation>

  Neither <math|\<cal-D\>*\<varphi\>> nor the action make sense, since
  typical <math|\<varphi\>> are distributions. The Gaussian part, however,
  defines the Gaussian free field <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>> of
  Section<nbsp><hlink|3|cqft_notes_L2.tm#sec:gff>, and one is led to define
  <math|\<mu\>\<propto\>\<mathe\><rsup|-<big|int>V<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>>\<mu\><rsub|m>>
  with a renormalised <math|V>, at least in finite volume in <math|d=2>. In
  <math|d=3> this is possible only as the limit of ultraviolet-regularised
  measures of this form, and the limit is singular with respect to
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>>. Which measures on
  <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>|)>> correspond to
  relativistic QFTs? Let <math|<wide|\<mu\>|^><around|(|f|)>\<assign\><big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>>\<mu\><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>>
  be the characteristic functional and <math|\<Theta\>*<around|(|\<tau\>,<math-bf|x>|)>=<around|(|-\<tau\>,<math-bf|x>|)>>,
  <math|\<Theta\>*f=f\<circ\>\<Theta\>>.

  <\description-long>
    <item*|(OS0) Analyticity>For real <math|f<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,f<rsub|n>\<in\>C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|c><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>|)>>,
    the map <math|z\<mapsto\><wide|\<mu\>|^><around|(|<big|sum><rsub|j>z<rsub|j>*f<rsub|j>|)>>
    is entire on <math|\<bbb-C\><rsup|n>>.

    <item*|(OS1) Regularity>For some <math|p\<in\><around|[|1,2|]>> and
    <math|c\<gtr\>0>, <math|<around|\||<wide|\<mu\>|^><around|(|f|)>|\|>\<leq\>exp
    <around*|(|c*<around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|p>><rsup|p>|)>|)>>
    for all complex-valued <math|f\<in\>C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|c><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>|)>>,
    with <math|<wide|\<mu\>|^>> extended by (OS0); if <math|p=2>, the
    two-point function is in addition required to be locally integrable.

    <item*|(OS2) Euclidean invariance><math|<wide|\<mu\>|^><around|(|f\<circ\>g|)>=<wide|\<mu\>|^><around|(|f|)>>
    for every Euclidean motion <math|g> of <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>.

    <item*|(OS3) Reflection positivity>For real
    <math|f<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,f<rsub|n>> supported in
    <math|<around|{|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0|}>>, the matrix
    <math|<around*|(|<wide|\<mu\>|^>*<around|(|f<rsub|i>-\<Theta\>*f<rsub|j>|)>|)><rsub|i,j>>
    is positive semidefinite.

    <item*|(OS4) Ergodicity>The time translations act ergodically on
    <math|<around|(|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>|)>,\<mu\>|)>>.
  </description-long>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Osterwalder\USchrader reconstruction<nbsp><cite|OS73|OS75|GJ87>>If
    <math|\<mu\>> satisfies (OS0)\U(OS3), its moments are the Schwinger
    functions of a unique Wightman theory satisfying (W1)\U(W4) and (W0)
    except possibly uniqueness of the vacuum. (OS4) holds iff the vacuum is
    unique.
  </theorem>

  <\render-proof|Idea>
    Let <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|+>> be the functions of <math|\<varphi\>> that
    depend only on its restriction to <math|<around|{|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0|}>>,
    with <math|<around|(|F,G|)>\<assign\>\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|[|<wide|\<Theta\>*F|\<bar\>>*<space|0.17em>G|]>>.
    By (OS3) this is a positive semi-definite form; quotienting by null
    vectors and completing gives <math|\<cal-H\>>. Time translations by
    <math|t\<geq\>0> map <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|+>> to itself and descend to a
    self-adjoint contraction semigroup <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-t*H>>, so
    <math|H\<geq\>0>: <em|positivity of the energy is recovered from
    reflection positivity>. The vacuum is the class of <math|1>, and the
    analytic continuation <math|\<tau\>\<to\>\<mathi\>t> yields the Wightman
    functions.
  </render-proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Positivity, clustering and uniqueness of the
    vacuum><label|rem:clustering> Let <math|H\<geq\>0> be self-adjoint,
    <math|\<Omega\>\<in\>ker H>, and let <math|P<rsub|0>> be the orthogonal
    projection onto <math|ker H>. Positivity of the energy turns uniqueness
    of the vacuum into a clustering statement, in three steps.

    (i) <em|Imaginary time: no averaging is needed.> By the spectral theorem
    and dominated convergence, <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-t*H>=<big|int><rsub|<around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>>\<mathe\><rsup|-t*\<lambda\>>\<mathd\>E<around|(|\<lambda\>|)>\<to\>E<around|(|<around|{|0|}>|)>=P<rsub|0>>
    strongly as <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>, because
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-t*\<lambda\>>\<to\><with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|\<lambda\>=0>>
    for <math|\<lambda\>\<geq\>0>. In the reconstruction above, for
    <math|F,G\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|+>> and <math|\<tau\><rsub|t>> the time
    translation,

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>>*<around*|[|<wide|\<Theta\>*F|\<bar\>>*<space|0.17em>\<tau\><rsub|t>*G|]>=<around|(|F,\<mathe\><rsup|-t*H>G|)><long-arrow|\<rubber-rightarrow\>|t\<to\>\<infty\>|><around|(|F,P<rsub|0>*G|)>.
    </equation*>

    So the Euclidean correlations cluster,
    <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|[|<wide|\<Theta\>*F|\<bar\>>*<space|0.17em>\<tau\><rsub|t>*G|]>\<to\><wide|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>>*F|\<bar\>><space|0.17em>\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>>*G>,
    iff <math|P<rsub|0>> is the projection onto <math|\<bbb-C\>*\<Omega\>>,
    i.e. iff the vacuum is unique. With a mass gap the convergence is
    exponential, at rate <math|inf <around|(|<math-up|spec>H\<setminus\><around|{|0|}>|)>>
    (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:vacuum>). Moreover, the spectrum condition
    <math|H\<geq\><around|\||<math-bf|P>|\|>> implies that a vector with
    <math|H*\<psi\>=0> also has <math|<math-bf|P>\<psi\>=0>: invariance under
    time translations already gives invariance under all translations. For
    reflection positive measures, ergodicity (OS4) (by the reconstruction
    theorem) and clustering in imaginary time (by (i)) are both equivalent to
    uniqueness of the vacuum.

    (ii) <em|Real time: only averages converge.> The unitary group
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>t*H>> does not converge as
    <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>> in general. Only its time averages do:
    <math|<frac|1|T>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|T>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>t*H>\<mathd\>t\<to\>P<rsub|0>>
    strongly (von Neumann's mean ergodic theorem). If <math|H> has an
    eigenvalue <math|E\<gtr\>0> with eigenvector <math|\<psi\>>, then
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>t*H>\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>=\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>t*E><around|\<\|\|\>|\<psi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>
    oscillates forever, while <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,\<mathe\><rsup|-t*H>\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>=\<mathe\><rsup|-t*E><around|\<\|\|\>|\<psi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<to\>0>.
    This is the situation of the lattice systems of
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:euclid> in finite volume: their real-time
    correlations never decay, although the vacuum is unique and the Euclidean
    correlations cluster exponentially.

    (iii) <em|Relativistic theories.> Here Lorentz covariance removes the
    eigenvalues. If <math|U*<around|(|a,<with|math-font|Bbb|1>|)>*\<psi\>=\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>p\<cdot\>a>\<psi\>>
    with <math|p\<neq\>0>, then <math|U*<around|(|0,\<Lambda\>|)>*\<psi\>> is
    an eigenvector with eigenvalue <math|\<Lambda\>*p>. The orbit of <math|p>
    is uncountable and eigenvectors for distinct eigenvalues are orthogonal,
    which contradicts the separability of <math|\<cal-H\>>. So, if the vacuum
    is unique, the spectral measures of the translations on
    <math|\<Omega\><rsup|\<perp\>>> have no atoms, and by Wiener's theorem
    correlations cluster in mean:

    <\equation*>
      lim<rsub|R\<to\>\<infty\>> <frac|1|<around|\||B<rsub|R>|\|>>*<big|int><rsub|B<rsub|R>><around*|\||<around|\<langle\>|\<chi\>,U*<around|(|a,<with|math-font|Bbb|1>|)>*\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>-<around|\<langle\>|\<chi\>,\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>><around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>|\|><rsup|2>\<mathd\>a=0.
    </equation*>

    Locality then upgrades this to the pointwise cluster property (e) in
    spacelike directions<nbsp><cite|SW64>. With a mass gap, the truncated
    functions decay faster than any power (Ruelle<nbsp><cite|Rue62>), and in
    fact exponentially (Araki, Hepp and Ruelle<nbsp><cite|AHR62>);
    see<nbsp><cite-detail|Haa96|ŸII.4>. Conversely, if <math|ker H> contains
    a unit vector <math|\<psi\>\<perp\>\<Omega\>>, then by cyclicity (W4)
    some product of fields has a nonzero component along <math|\<psi\>>, and
    the corresponding correlations do not factorise. In Euclidean terms the
    measure is then a mixture of several ergodic components, or phases, as
    for <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>> at strong coupling with its
    symmetric mixture of the two pure phases<nbsp><cite|GJS75>.
  </remark>

  <paragraph|The Euclidean strategy.> (1) Regularise: lattice spacing
  <math|\<varepsilon\>> (or another UV cutoff), finite volume. (2)
  Renormalise: choose <math|\<varepsilon\>>-dependent counterterms. (3) Prove
  uniform estimates and remove the cutoffs. (4) Verify the OS axioms:
  reflection positivity survives limits of lattice approximations; Euclidean
  invariance must be recovered in the limit; (OS1) needs uniform bounds;
  (OS4) often follows from a mass gap. (5) Show the limit is not Gaussian. In
  the next lectures we follow this route using stochastic analysis and PDE
  methods, starting from the Gaussian free field.

  <subsection*|Exercises>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Lattice equations of motion><label|ex:heisenberg> Derive
    <eqref|eq:latticewave> from <eqref|eq:Hlat>. For <math|V=0>, diagonalise
    <math|H<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> in Fourier modes
    <math|k\<in\><frac|2*\<pi\>|L>*\<bbb-Z\>\<cap\><around|(|-<frac|\<pi\>|\<varepsilon\>>,<frac|\<pi\>|\<varepsilon\>>|]>>
    and show that the frequencies are <math|\<omega\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|k|)><rsup|2>=m<rsup|2>+<frac|4|\<varepsilon\><rsup|2>>*sin<rsup|2>
    <frac|k*\<varepsilon\>|2>>. Deduce that
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,\<varphi\><rsub|x><rsup|2>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|\<rangle\>>=<frac|1|L>*<big|sum><rsub|k><frac|1|2*\<omega\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|k|)>>=<frac|1|2*\<pi\>>*log
    <frac|1|\<varepsilon\>*m>+O<around|(|1|)>>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Harmonic oscillator and Ornstein\UUhlenbeck><label|ex:OU> Let
    <math|H=-<frac|1|2>*\<partial\><rsub|q><rsup|2>+<frac|1|2>*m<rsup|2>*q<rsup|2>>
    on <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<bbb-R\>|)>>. (a) Show that
    <math|\<Omega\><around|(|q|)>=<around|(|m/\<pi\>|)><rsup|1/4>\<mathe\><rsup|-m*q<rsup|2>/2>>
    and <math|E<rsub|0>=m/2>. (b) Show that
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,q<space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<tau\>*<wide|H|\<bar\>>>q*<space|0.17em>\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=\<mathe\><rsup|-m*\<tau\>>/<around|(|2*m|)>>.
    (c) Let <math|<around|(|U*f|)><around|(|q|)>=\<Omega\><around|(|q|)>*f<around|(|q|)>>,
    unitary from <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<Omega\><rsup|2>\<mathd\>q|)>> to
    <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<mathd\>q|)>>. Show that
    <math|U<rsup|-1>*<wide|H|\<bar\>>*U=-<frac|1|2>*\<partial\><rsub|q><rsup|2>+m*q*\<partial\><rsub|q>>,
    minus the generator of <math|\<mathd\>X=-m*X\<mathd\>t+\<mathd\>B>. (d)
    Conclude that the Schwinger functions are the moments of the stationary
    Gaussian process with covariance <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-m*<around|\||\<tau\>-\<sigma\>|\|>>/<around|(|2*m|)>=<around|(|-\<partial\><rsub|\<tau\>><rsup|2>+m<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>*<around|(|\<tau\>,\<sigma\>|)>>:
    the GFF in <math|d=1>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Lattice action in general dimension><label|ex:lattice-action>
    Verify <eqref|eq:transfer>\U<eqref|eq:FK-lattice>. In spacetime dimension
    <math|d> (lattice <math|\<varepsilon\>*\<bbb-Z\><rsup|d-1>> in space,
    <math|\<delta\>=\<varepsilon\>>) show that
    <math|S<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=\<varepsilon\><rsup|d-2>*<big|sum><rsub|<around|\<langle\>|u,v|\<rangle\>>><frac|1|2>*<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|u>-\<varphi\><rsub|v>|)><rsup|2>+\<varepsilon\><rsup|d>*<big|sum><rsub|u><around|[|<frac|1|2>*m<rsup|2>*\<varphi\><rsub|u><rsup|2>+V<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|u>|)>|]>>,
    so that <math|\<varepsilon\><rsup|<around|(|d-2|)>/2>*\<varphi\>> is the
    natural dimensionless spin variable.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Correlation length and criticality><label|ex:corrlength> On
    <math|\<varepsilon\>*\<bbb-Z\><rsup|d>> consider the lattice Green
    function

    <\equation*>
      \<Gamma\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|x|)>=<big|int><rsub|<around|[|-\<pi\>/\<varepsilon\>,\<pi\>/\<varepsilon\>|]><rsup|d>><frac|\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>k\<cdot\>x>|m<rsup|2>+4*\<varepsilon\><rsup|-2>*<big|sum><rsub|j>sin<rsup|2><around|(|\<varepsilon\>*k<rsub|j>/2|)>>*<space|0.17em><frac|\<mathd\>k|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|d>>.
    </equation*>

    (a) Show that <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|x|)>\<to\>G<rsub|m><around|(|x|)>>
    for <math|x\<neq\>0> (with <math|x\<in\>\<varepsilon\>*\<bbb-Z\><rsup|d>>
    along a sequence <math|\<varepsilon\>\<to\>0>, e.g. dyadic), where
    <math|G<rsub|m>> is the Green function of <math|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>> on
    <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>. (b) Show that in lattice units
    <math|n=x/\<varepsilon\>> along a coordinate axis,
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> decays like
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mu\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|\||n|\|>>> with
    <math|cosh \<mu\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=1+<frac|1|2>*<around|(|\<varepsilon\>*m|)><rsup|2>>.
    Deduce that the lattice correlation length is
    <math|\<xi\>=\<mu\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|-1>\<sim\><around|(|\<varepsilon\>*m|)><rsup|-1>\<to\>\<infty\>>
    and interpret.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Reflection positivity on the lattice><label|ex:RP-lattice> Let
    <math|\<Lambda\>=<around|(|\<varepsilon\>*\<bbb-Z\>/T*\<bbb-Z\>|)>\<times\>\<Lambda\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>
    with <math|T=2*K*\<varepsilon\>>, let <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>
    have action <eqref|eq:spin-system>, and let <math|\<Theta\>> be the
    reflection <math|\<tau\>\<mapsto\>-\<tau\>>, which fixes the planes
    <math|\<tau\>=0> and <math|\<tau\>=T/2>. Show that
    <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|F\<cdot\>\<Theta\>*F|]>\<geq\>0> for every
    bounded real <math|F> depending only on
    <math|<around|{|\<varphi\><rsub|u>:\<tau\><around|(|u|)>\<in\><around|[|0,T/2|]>|}>>.
    <em|Hint:> write <math|S<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=S<rsub|+>+\<Theta\>*S<rsub|+>>,
    with the terms living on the fixed planes split evenly, and integrate
    first over the sites off the fixed planes.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Vacuum, mass gap and clustering><label|ex:vacuum> Let <math|H> be
    self-adjoint with <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-t*H>> trace class for
    <math|t\<gtr\>0>, simple ground state <math|\<Omega\>>, and
    <math|E<rsub|1>\<assign\>min <around|(|<math-up|spec>H\<setminus\><around|{|E<rsub|0>|}>|)>>.
    (a) Show that <math|<math-up|Tr><around|(|A\<mathe\><rsup|-T*H>|)>/<math-up|Tr><around|(|\<mathe\><rsup|-T*H>|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,A*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>+O<around|(|\<mathe\><rsup|-T*<around|(|E<rsub|1>-E<rsub|0>|)>>|)>>
    for bounded <math|A>. (b) Show that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,A\<mathe\><rsup|-\<tau\>*<wide|H|\<bar\>>>B*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>-<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,A*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>><around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,B*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>|\|>\<leq\><around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>><around|\<\|\|\>|B|\<\|\|\>>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<tau\>*<around|(|E<rsub|1>-E<rsub|0>|)>>:
    </equation*>

    a mass gap gives exponential clustering of Schwinger functions.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|A toy OS reconstruction><label|ex:toyOS> Let <math|\<mu\>> be a
    probability measure on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|\<varepsilon\>*\<bbb-Z\>\<times\>\<Lambda\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>>,
    invariant under time translations and reflection positive with respect to
    <math|\<Theta\>>. On bounded functions <math|F> of
    <math|<around|{|\<varphi\><rsub|u>:\<tau\><around|(|u|)>\<geq\>0|}>> put
    <math|<around|(|F,G|)>=\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|<wide|\<Theta\>*F|\<bar\>>*G|]>>
    and <math|<around|(|<math-ss|T>F|)><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=F<around|(|\<varphi\>|(>\<cdot\>+\<varepsilon\>,\<cdot\><around|)||)>>.
    Show that <math|<around|(|F,<math-ss|T>G|)>=<around|(|<math-ss|T>F,G|)>>
    and, by iterating Cauchy\USchwarz, <math|<around|\||<around|(|F,<math-ss|T>F|)>|\|>\<leq\><around|(|F,F|)>>.
    Deduce that <math|<math-ss|T>> induces a self-adjoint contraction on the
    reconstructed Hilbert space, and that
    <math|H\<assign\>-<around|(|2*\<varepsilon\>|)><rsup|-1>*log
    <math-ss|T><rsup|2>\<geq\>0> on <math|<around|(|ker
    <math-ss|T>|)><rsup|\<perp\>>>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Positivity, clustering and uniqueness><label|ex:clustering> (a)
    Let <math|H\<geq\>0> be self-adjoint. Show that
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-t*H>\<to\>P<rsub|0>> strongly as
    <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>, where <math|P<rsub|0>> is the projection onto
    <math|ker H>, and that <math|<frac|1|T>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|T>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>t*H>\<mathd\>t\<to\>P<rsub|0>>
    strongly. Give an example where <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>t*H>> has
    no weak limit. (b) In the setting of Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:toyOS>,
    show that <math|\<bbb-E\><around|[|<wide|\<Theta\>*F|\<bar\>><space|0.17em><math-ss|T><rsup|2*n>G|]>\<to\><wide|\<bbb-E\>*F|\<bar\>>*<space|0.17em>\<bbb-E\>*G>
    for all <math|F,G> iff the <math|<math-ss|T><rsup|2>>-invariant vectors
    of the reconstructed Hilbert space are the multiples of the vacuum. Why
    <math|<math-ss|T><rsup|2>>? Find a reflection positive, translation
    invariant measure for which <math|<math-ss|T>> has the eigenvalue
    <math|-1>. (c) Under (W0), show that <math|H*\<psi\>=0> implies
    <math|U*<around|(|a,<with|math-font|Bbb|1>|)>*\<psi\>=\<psi\>> for all
    <math|a>. (d) Under (W0), show that the translations have no eigenvector
    with eigenvalue <math|p\<neq\>0>. <em|Hint:> boost it. (e) Let
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<pm\>>> be two distinct, ergodic, translation
    invariant, reflection positive measures and
    <math|\<mu\>=<frac|1|2>*<around|(|\<mu\><rsub|+>+\<mu\><rsub|->|)>>. Show
    that <math|\<mu\>> is reflection positive but not ergodic, and that its
    reconstructed Hilbert space contains two orthogonal invariant vectors.
  </exercise>

  <section|The course><label|app:plan>

  These notes cover the first of six two-hour lectures given at SISSA,
  Trieste, in October\UNovember 2026, for graduate students in analysis,
  probability or mathematical physics. Lectures 1\U3 have their own notes;
  the content of Lectures 4\U6 is not yet settled. They will probably be
  devoted to a construction of <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> on the torus
  via the FBSDE of Lecture 3.

  <paragraph|Prerequisites.> Measure-theoretic probability and Itô calculus;
  the spectral theorem for self-adjoint operators; distributions and Fourier
  analysis. No previous knowledge of quantum field theory is assumed.

  <paragraph|Approach.> The course follows the Euclidean strategy of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:euclid>. After the Gaussian free field,
  interacting theories are studied through the scale decomposition of the
  field: the Polchinski equation, its probabilistic form as a
  forward\Ubackward stochastic differential equation (FBSDE) for the field at
  increasing resolution, and the associated variational problem. Stochastic
  quantisation, cluster expansions and correlation inequalities appear as
  complementary approaches.

  <big-table|<with|par-mode|center|font-size|0.84|<tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|4|4|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|Lecture>|<cell|Main
  results>>|<row|<cell|1>|<cell|Relativistic quantum fields and imaginary
  time>|<cell|No fields at a point; Källén\ULehmann; Wick rotation;
  Feynman\UKac; OS axioms>>|<row|<cell|2>|<cell|The Gaussian free
  field>|<cell|Construction; reflection positivity; Wick powers; DS
  characterisation; parabolic and elliptic
  SQ>>|<row|<cell|3>|<cell|Stochastic observables, the Polchinski flow and
  FBSDEs>|<cell|Polchinski equation and FBSDE; force coordinates and the
  Wetterich equation; Boué\UDupuis formula>>>>><label|tab:course>>|Lectures
  1\U3.>

  <paragraph|Lecture 1. Relativistic quantum fields and imaginary time>
  (Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:wightman> and <reference|sec:euclid>).
  Wightman axioms; no fields at a point; Källén\ULehmann representation;
  quantum wave equation and its problems; lattice Hamiltonian on a circle;
  Wick rotation from positivity of the energy; Feynman\UKac formula; lattice
  spin systems, continuum limit <math|=> critical point; OS axioms and
  reconstruction (statement and idea). <em|Proofs in class:>
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:nopoint>, Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:KL>
  (sketch), Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:wick-rot>. <em|Exercises:>
  Exercises<nbsp><reference|ex:restrictions>, <reference|ex:gen-free>,
  <reference|ex:OU>, <reference|ex:corrlength> and <reference|ex:RP-lattice>.
  <em|Reading:> <cite|SW64|BSZ92|GJ87|Sim79>.

  <paragraph|Lecture 2. The Gaussian free field>
  (Section<nbsp><hlink|3|cqft_notes_L2.tm#sec:gff>). Construction on
  <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d>> and <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>; reflection
  positivity and the free Markov field; Euclidean Källén\ULehmann; Wick
  powers; Gaussian integration by parts; DS characterisation; parabolic and
  elliptic stochastic quantisation. <em|Proofs in class:>
  Theorem<nbsp><hlink|3.2|cqft_notes_L2.tm#thm:existence>,
  Proposition<nbsp><hlink|3.4|cqft_notes_L2.tm#prop:RP>,
  Theorem<nbsp><hlink|3.9|cqft_notes_L2.tm#thm:wick>,
  Theorem<nbsp><hlink|3.16|cqft_notes_L2.tm#thm:DS-unique>,
  Theorem<nbsp><hlink|3.19|cqft_notes_L2.tm#thm:parabolic>,
  Theorem<nbsp><hlink|3.21|cqft_notes_L2.tm#thm:elliptic>. <em|Exercises:>
  Exercises<nbsp><hlink|3.3|cqft_notes_L2.tm#ex:regularity>,
  <hlink|3.6|cqft_notes_L2.tm#ex:wickexp>,
  <hlink|3.7|cqft_notes_L2.tm#ex:wick-thm>,
  <hlink|3.8|cqft_notes_L2.tm#ex:DS-finite>,
  <hlink|3.2|cqft_notes_L2.tm#ex:KL-euclid> and
  <hlink|3.13|cqft_notes_L2.tm#ex:slices>. <em|Reading:>
  <cite|She07|Jan97|DPZ14|ADG20>.

  <paragraph|Lecture 3. Stochastic observables, the Polchinski flow and
  FBSDEs> (separate notes). Scale decomposition of the GFF and stochastic
  observables; effective potentials and the Polchinski equation; the
  Polchinski process as an FBSDE whose force is a martingale; the FBSDE in
  force coordinates, where the transport term is integrated exactly, and the
  Wetterich equation for the effective average action; the Boué\UDupuis
  formula, with the Polchinski equation as its Hamilton\UJacobi\UBellman
  equation, in finite and infinite volume. Additional material: Wick powers
  as observables in every dimension and the operator product expansion;
  perturbation theory and counterterms; Dyson\USchwinger equations at every
  scale. <em|Reading:> <cite|Pol84|BBD24|BD98|BG20|BCG26>.

  <paragraph|Homework and projects.> Problem sets can be drawn from the
  exercises at the end of each section. The computer experiments of
  Section<nbsp><hlink|3|cqft_notes_L2.tm#sec:gff> (lattice GFF, lattice
  stochastic quantisation) and the zero-dimensional Polchinski process of
  Lecture 3 can be extended to interacting models and are suitable as small
  projects. Possible reading projects: the Griffiths\USimon construction
  <cite|GS73>; dimensional reduction <cite|KLP84|ADG20>; the Euclidean
  approach to the particle structure of <math|P<around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsub|2>>
  <cite|GJS74>.

  <section*|References and further reading>

  <paragraph|Guide.> <em|Axioms and general structure:>
  <cite|SW64|BSZ92|Haa96|RS75>; the point-field result is from <cite|Wig64>;
  the spectral representation of the two-point function from
  <cite|Kal52|Leh54>; particle structure of
  <math|P<around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsub|2>> in <cite|GJS74>. <em|Hamiltonian
  construction of <math|P<around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsub|2>> in finite volume:>
  <cite|Nel66|GJ68|Seg70|BSZ92>. <em|Euclidean field theory and
  reconstruction:> <cite|Sym69|Nel73a|Nel73b|OS73|OS75>; the textbooks
  <cite|GJ87|Sim74|Sim79> are the standard references for everything in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:euclid>. <em|Lattice approximations and
  statistical mechanics:> <cite|GRS75|GS73|WK74|Bry09|BBS19|ADC21>.
  <em|Surveys of the constructive programme:> <cite|Jaf00|Sum12>.

