    <\associate|figure>
      <tuple|normal|<surround|<hidden-binding|<tuple>|1.1>||Minkowski space
      for <with|mode|<quote|math>|s=1>. The shaded regions are timelike to
      the origin. The supports of <with|mode|<quote|math>|f> and
      <with|mode|<quote|math>|g> are spacelike separated, so
      <with|mode|<quote|math>|<around|[|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>,\<varphi\><around|(|g|)>|]>=0>
      by (W3). In momentum space the same forward cone
      <with|mode|<quote|math>|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|<space|-0.17em>+>>
      carries the joint spectrum of <with|mode|<quote|math>|<around|(|H,<with|mode|<quote|text>|<with|font-family|<quote|rm>|font-series|<quote|bold>|font-shape|<quote|right>|P>>|)>>.>|<pageref|auto-6>>

      <tuple|normal|<surround|<hidden-binding|<tuple>|1.2>||Typical spectral
      measure of an interacting field with mass gap (Theorem
      <no-break><specific|screen|<resize|<move|<with|color|<quote|#A0A0FF>|->|-0.3em|>|0em||0em|>><reference|thm:KL>
      and Remark <no-break><specific|screen|<resize|<move|<with|color|<quote|#A0A0FF>|->|-0.3em|>|0em||0em|>><reference|rem:KL>):
      an isolated atom at the one-particle mass
      (<with|mode|<quote|math>|0\<less\>Z\<leq\>1> for a canonical field),
      and a continuum starting at <with|mode|<quote|math>|<around|(|2*m|)><rsup|2>>,
      or at <with|mode|<quote|math>|<around|(|3*m|)><rsup|2>> in the presence
      of the symmetry <with|mode|<quote|math>|\<varphi\>\<mapsto\>-\<varphi\>>.
      The free field has <with|mode|<quote|math>|\<rho\>=\<delta\><rsub|m<rsup|2>>>.>|<pageref|auto-11>>

      <tuple|normal|<surround|<hidden-binding|<tuple>|2.1>||Wick rotation
      (Proposition <no-break><specific|screen|<resize|<move|<with|color|<quote|#A0A0FF>|->|-0.3em|>|0em||0em|>><reference|prop:wick-rot>).
      Since <with|mode|<quote|math>|<wide|H|\<bar\>>\<geq\>0>,
      <with|mode|<quote|math>|\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>\<zeta\>*<wide|H|\<bar\>>>>
      is a contraction on the lower half-plane
      <with|mode|<quote|math>|<with|mode|<quote|text>|<with|font-family|<quote|rm>|font-shape|<quote|right>|Im>>\<zeta\>\<leq\>0>,
      analytic in its interior. The Wightman functions on the real axis and
      the Schwinger functions on the negative imaginary axis are boundary
      values and restrictions of the same analytic
      function.>|<pageref|auto-19>>

      <tuple|normal|<surround|<hidden-binding|<tuple>|2.2>||Left: a sample of
      the stationary Ornstein\UUhlenbeck process, the imaginary-time path of
      one harmonic oscillator, i.e. the GFF in <with|mode|<quote|math>|d=1>
      (Exercise <no-break><specific|screen|<resize|<move|<with|color|<quote|#A0A0FF>|->|-0.3em|>|0em||0em|>><reference|ex:OU>).
      Right: its empirical two-point function against the Schwinger function
      <with|mode|<quote|math>|\<mathe\><rsup|-m*\<tau\>>/2*m>.>|<pageref|auto-21>>

      <tuple|normal|<surround|<hidden-binding|<tuple>|2.3>||The Euclidean
      space-time lattice. Rows are configurations of the quantum system at
      fixed imaginary time; the transfer matrix
      <with|mode|<quote|math>|T<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<approx\>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<varepsilon\>*H<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>>
      connects consecutive rows. The nearest-neighbour structure makes the
      measure reflection positive with respect to
      <with|mode|<quote|math>|\<Theta\>>.>|<pageref|auto-23>>
    </associate>
